/* PostgreSQL Mastery — Interview bank part 2
   Sections: postgresql (100), architecture (100), performance (100), scenario (100).
   Target: PostgreSQL 16–18. Extensions and third-party tools are labelled as such. */
PGM.INTERVIEW = PGM.INTERVIEW || {};

PGM.INTERVIEW.postgresql = [
{ q: `What is MVCC in PostgreSQL and how is it implemented physically?`,
  a: `<p>Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) lets readers and writers not block each other: every transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data. PostgreSQL implements it by storing <strong>multiple physical row versions (tuples)</strong> in the table heap itself. Each tuple header carries <code>xmin</code> (the transaction ID that created it) and <code>xmax</code> (the transaction ID that deleted or locked it).</p><p>An <code>UPDATE</code> never overwrites in place: it marks the old tuple's <code>xmax</code> and inserts a new tuple. A snapshot (xmin, xmax, list of in-progress XIDs) plus the commit log (<code>pg_xact</code>) decides which version is visible. The cost of this design is dead tuples, which <code>VACUUM</code> must later reclaim — unlike Oracle/MySQL InnoDB, which keep old versions in a separate undo log.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','internals'] },
{ q: `What are xmin and xmax, and how can you see them?`,
  a: `<p><code>xmin</code> and <code>xmax</code> are hidden system columns on every heap tuple. <code>xmin</code> is the XID of the inserting transaction; <code>xmax</code> is 0 for a live, unlocked row, or the XID that deleted/updated it (or that holds a row lock on it). You can select them explicitly:</p><pre>SELECT xmin, xmax, ctid, id, name FROM departments LIMIT 3;</pre><p>Other system columns are <code>ctid</code> (physical location: block, offset), <code>cmin</code>/<code>cmax</code> (command IDs within a transaction) and <code>tableoid</code>. Note that a non-zero <code>xmax</code> does not always mean deleted — <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> also sets it, with infomask bits saying it is a lock only.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','system-columns'] },
{ q: `What is a ctid and why should applications never rely on it?`,
  a: `<p><code>ctid</code> is the tuple's physical address: <code>(block_number, line_pointer)</code> in the heap file. It is useful for diagnostics and for one-off de-duplication (<code>DELETE ... WHERE ctid NOT IN (...)</code>), but it is <strong>not a stable identifier</strong>: any <code>UPDATE</code> creates a new tuple with a new ctid, and <code>VACUUM FULL</code>, <code>CLUSTER</code> or <code>pg_repack</code> rewrite the table and change every ctid. Use a primary key for identity.</p>`,
  tags: ['internals','storage'] },
{ q: `Why does PostgreSQL need VACUUM at all?`,
  a: `<p>Because MVCC leaves dead tuples in the heap after <code>UPDATE</code>/<code>DELETE</code>/aborted inserts. <code>VACUUM</code> does four jobs:</p><ul><li>Removes dead tuples and their index entries so space is reusable (recorded in the Free Space Map).</li><li>Updates the <strong>Visibility Map</strong> so index-only scans can skip heap visits and later vacuums can skip all-visible pages.</li><li><strong>Freezes</strong> old tuples so transaction IDs can wrap around safely.</li><li>Updates <code>pg_class.relpages/reltuples</code> estimates (and with <code>ANALYZE</code>, column statistics).</li></ul><p>Plain <code>VACUUM</code> does not return space to the OS (except truncating empty pages at the end of the file) and runs alongside reads and writes with a <code>SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE</code> lock.</p>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','mvcc'] },
{ q: `VACUUM vs VACUUM FULL — what is the difference?`,
  a: `<p><strong>VACUUM</strong> marks dead space as reusable inside the existing file, takes <code>SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE</code> (DML continues), and is what autovacuum runs. <strong>VACUUM FULL</strong> rewrites the whole table and its indexes into new files, returning space to the OS, but takes an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock — no reads or writes — and needs free disk roughly equal to the table plus indexes.</p><p>In production, prefer avoiding bloat via tuned autovacuum; when a rewrite is truly needed online, use the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pg_repack</code> (or pg_squeeze), which rebuilds with only brief locks.</p>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','bloat','locks'] },
{ q: `How does autovacuum decide when to vacuum a table?`,
  a: `<p>The autovacuum launcher wakes every <code>autovacuum_naptime</code> (1 min) and workers vacuum a table when:</p><pre>dead tuples &gt; autovacuum_vacuum_threshold (50)
              + autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor (0.2) * reltuples</pre><p>Also an insert-based trigger (PG 13+): <code>autovacuum_vacuum_insert_threshold</code> (1000) + <code>autovacuum_vacuum_insert_scale_factor</code> (0.2) × reltuples, so append-only tables get frozen and visibility-mapped. ANALYZE uses <code>autovacuum_analyze_threshold</code> + <code>autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor</code> (0.1). Independently, a table whose <code>relfrozenxid</code> age exceeds <code>autovacuum_freeze_max_age</code> (200M) gets an anti-wraparound vacuum even if autovacuum is disabled. PG 18 adds <code>autovacuum_vacuum_max_threshold</code> (default 100M) to cap the dead-tuple trigger on huge tables.</p>`,
  tags: ['vacuum','autovacuum','gucs'] },
{ q: `Why is the default autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor of 0.2 a problem for large tables, and how do you fix it?`,
  a: `<p>For a 500M-row table, 0.2 means autovacuum waits for ~100M dead tuples before starting — by then the table is bloated, the vacuum takes hours and index scans have degraded. Fix it <strong>per table</strong> with storage parameters rather than globally:</p><pre>ALTER TABLE orders SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.01,
                        autovacuum_vacuum_threshold = 10000,
                        autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor = 0.02);</pre><p>Also raise throughput: <code>autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit</code> (default -1 means use <code>vacuum_cost_limit</code>=200, shared across workers) and possibly <code>autovacuum_max_workers</code>. PG 18's <code>autovacuum_vacuum_max_threshold</code> provides a global cap as well.</p>`,
  tags: ['autovacuum','tuning'] },
{ q: `What is transaction ID wraparound?`,
  a: `<p>XIDs are 32-bit and compared using modulo-2<sup>32</sup> arithmetic: any XID sees ~2.1 billion XIDs as "in the past" and ~2.1 billion as "in the future". If an old tuple's <code>xmin</code> were left unfrozen for more than ~2<sup>31</sup> transactions, it would suddenly appear to be in the future and the row would vanish. To prevent this, <code>VACUUM</code> <strong>freezes</strong> old tuples (sets a frozen infomask bit), and the database tracks <code>pg_class.relfrozenxid</code> and <code>pg_database.datfrozenxid</code>.</p><p>Safeguards: forced anti-wraparound autovacuum at <code>autovacuum_freeze_max_age</code> (200M), the failsafe at <code>vacuum_failsafe_age</code> (1.6B) which disables cost delay and skips index cleanup, warnings at 40M XIDs remaining, and refusal to assign new XIDs at 3M remaining.</p>`,
  tags: ['wraparound','vacuum','freeze'] },
{ q: `How do you monitor how close a database is to XID wraparound?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid) AS xid_age,
       round(100.0 * age(datfrozenxid) / 2000000000, 1) AS pct_to_limit
FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC;

SELECT c.oid::regclass, age(c.relfrozenxid) AS xid_age,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size(c.oid))
FROM pg_class c WHERE c.relkind IN ('r','m','t')
ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 20;</pre><p>Alert well before <code>autovacuum_freeze_max_age</code> (e.g. at 500M–1B). Also check <code>mxid_age(datminmxid)</code> for MultiXact wraparound, which has its own limits. Typical blockers of freezing: long-running transactions, abandoned replication slots, prepared transactions and <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> from a lagging replica.</p>`,
  tags: ['wraparound','monitoring'] },
{ q: `What are MultiXacts?`,
  a: `<p>A MultiXact ID represents a <strong>group of transactions</strong> that simultaneously lock the same row, e.g. several sessions doing <code>SELECT ... FOR SHARE</code>, or a <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code> lock taken by foreign-key checks while another transaction updates the row. Because <code>xmax</code> can hold only one ID, PostgreSQL stores a MultiXact ID there and keeps member lists in <code>pg_multixact/</code>.</p><p>MultiXacts have their own 32-bit space and wraparound (<code>autovacuum_multixact_freeze_max_age</code>, default 400M). Heavy FK-check workloads can generate lots of them; PG 17 made the SLRU caches configurable (<code>multixact_offset_buffers</code>, <code>multixact_member_buffers</code>) to reduce contention.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','locks','internals'] },
{ q: `What is the Write-Ahead Log (WAL) and what guarantees does it provide?`,
  a: `<p>The WAL is an append-only log of every change to data files. The rule: a change's WAL record must be flushed to durable storage <strong>before</strong> the modified data page is written. At <code>COMMIT</code>, PostgreSQL only needs to <code>fsync</code> the WAL (sequential I/O), not the scattered data pages.</p><p>This gives <strong>durability and crash recovery</strong> (replay WAL from the last checkpoint), and the same stream powers <strong>streaming replication</strong>, <strong>point-in-time recovery</strong> (archived WAL) and <strong>logical decoding</strong>. WAL lives in <code>pg_wal/</code> as 16 MB segment files by default.</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','durability'] },
{ q: `What is an LSN?`,
  a: `<p>A Log Sequence Number is a 64-bit byte position in the WAL stream, shown as <code>16/B374D848</code>. Each data page header stores the LSN of the last WAL record that modified it, which is how recovery knows whether a record still needs replaying. LSNs are used everywhere for progress tracking:</p><pre>SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn();
SELECT pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind
FROM pg_stat_replication;</pre>`,
  tags: ['wal','replication'] },
{ q: `What happens during a checkpoint?`,
  a: `<p>A checkpoint writes all dirty pages in shared buffers to the data files, fsyncs them, and writes a checkpoint record to WAL; <code>pg_control</code> then points at it. Crash recovery only needs to replay WAL from the last checkpoint's redo point, and older WAL segments can be recycled or removed.</p><p>Triggered by <code>checkpoint_timeout</code> (5 min default) or when WAL since the last checkpoint approaches <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB default). <code>checkpoint_completion_target</code> (0.9) spreads the writes over the interval to avoid I/O spikes. The first change to each page after a checkpoint writes a <strong>full-page image</strong> (<code>full_page_writes</code>) to protect against torn pages, which is why very frequent checkpoints inflate WAL volume.</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','checkpoint'] },
{ q: `What are full-page writes and why do they exist?`,
  a: `<p>The OS/disk writes in units (often 4 KB) smaller than PostgreSQL's 8 KB page, so a crash mid-write can leave a <strong>torn page</strong> half old, half new. WAL records normally store only deltas, which cannot be applied to a torn page. With <code>full_page_writes = on</code>, the first modification of each page after a checkpoint logs the entire page image, so recovery can restore it whole.</p><p>Consequences: WAL volume spikes right after each checkpoint; longer checkpoint intervals and <code>wal_compression</code> (pglz, lz4, zstd) reduce it. Only turn it off on storage that guarantees atomic 8 KB writes (e.g. some ZFS setups).</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','durability'] },
{ q: `Explain synchronous_commit levels.`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>off</code>: COMMIT returns before WAL is flushed locally; a crash can lose the last ~<code>3 × wal_writer_delay</code> of commits, but never corrupts data.</li><li><code>local</code>: wait for the local WAL flush only.</li><li><code>on</code> (default): local flush, plus — if <code>synchronous_standby_names</code> is set — the sync standby has flushed the WAL.</li><li><code>remote_write</code>: standby has written WAL to its OS (not fsynced).</li><li><code>remote_apply</code>: standby has replayed it, so reads on the standby see the commit.</li></ul><p>It can be set per transaction: e.g. <code>SET LOCAL synchronous_commit = off</code> for bulk click-stream inserts while payments keep full durability.</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','replication','gucs'] },
{ q: `What is TOAST?`,
  a: `<p>The Oversized-Attribute Storage Technique. A heap tuple must fit in a page, so when a row exceeds ~2 KB (<code>TOAST_TUPLE_THRESHOLD</code>), PostgreSQL first <strong>compresses</strong> large variable-length values (pglz or lz4) and, if still too big, moves them <strong>out of line</strong> into the table's TOAST relation (<code>pg_toast.pg_toast_&lt;oid&gt;</code>) as ~2 KB chunks, leaving a pointer in the main tuple. Max value size is 1 GB.</p><p>Benefits: queries that do not select the big column never read it; unchanged TOASTed values are not copied on <code>UPDATE</code>. Per-column strategy is controlled by <code>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET STORAGE PLAIN|MAIN|EXTERNAL|EXTENDED</code>, and compression by <code>SET COMPRESSION lz4</code> or <code>default_toast_compression</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['toast','storage'] },
{ q: `What are the TOAST storage strategies?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>PLAIN</code>: no compression, no out-of-line (fixed-length types like <code>int</code>).</li><li><code>EXTENDED</code>: compress, then move out of line if still large (default for <code>text</code>, <code>jsonb</code>, <code>bytea</code>).</li><li><code>EXTERNAL</code>: out of line but uncompressed — makes <code>substring()</code> on large <code>text</code>/<code>bytea</code> fast because only needed chunks are fetched.</li><li><code>MAIN</code>: compress, keep inline as long as possible.</li></ul><p>Example: <code>ALTER TABLE documents ALTER COLUMN body SET STORAGE EXTERNAL;</code> — only affects newly written values.</p>`,
  tags: ['toast','storage'] },
{ q: `Why can updating a single field inside a large JSONB document be expensive?`,
  a: `<p>JSONB values are immutable on disk: <code>jsonb_set()</code> produces a whole new value, and the <code>UPDATE</code> writes a new tuple. If the document is TOASTed, the entire document is decompressed, modified, recompressed and re-chunked into new TOAST rows, generating WAL proportional to the document size, plus dead TOAST chunks to vacuum. A 1 MB document updated 100 times per second means ~100 MB/s of WAL.</p><p>Mitigations: keep frequently mutated fields as normal columns, split hot and cold parts into separate tables, or keep documents small.</p>`,
  tags: ['jsonb','toast','performance'] },
{ q: `JSON vs JSONB — which should you use?`,
  a: `<p><code>json</code> stores the exact input text (whitespace, key order, duplicate keys) and reparses on every access. <code>jsonb</code> stores a decomposed binary form: keys sorted and de-duplicated (last wins), faster to query, and indexable with GIN (<code>jsonb_ops</code> or <code>jsonb_path_ops</code>) and supports containment (<code>@&gt;</code>), existence (<code>?</code>) and SQL/JSON path operators. Use <code>jsonb</code> almost always; use <code>json</code> only when you must preserve the exact original text (e.g. signed payloads, audit of raw input).</p>`,
  tags: ['jsonb','types'] },
{ q: `jsonb_ops vs jsonb_path_ops GIN operator classes?`,
  a: `<p><code>jsonb_ops</code> (default) indexes every key and value separately and supports <code>@&gt;</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>?|</code>, <code>?&amp;</code>, <code>@?</code> and <code>@@</code>. <code>jsonb_path_ops</code> indexes hashes of full paths-to-values, so it supports only <code>@&gt;</code>, <code>@?</code>, <code>@@</code> but is typically 2–3× smaller and faster for containment queries.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX ON products USING gin (attributes jsonb_path_ops);
SELECT * FROM products WHERE attributes @&gt; '{"brand":"Acme"}';</pre><p>If you always query one key, an expression B-tree index such as <code>((attributes-&gt;&gt;'brand'))</code> is smaller still.</p>`,
  tags: ['jsonb','gin','indexes'] },
{ q: `Describe PostgreSQL's process model.`,
  a: `<p>PostgreSQL is <strong>process-per-connection</strong>. The <code>postmaster</code> listens on the port and forks a dedicated <strong>backend</strong> process for every client connection. Background processes include: checkpointer, background writer, WAL writer, autovacuum launcher and workers, stats/logical replication launchers, WAL sender (per replica) and WAL receiver (on standbys), archiver, and parallel query workers. PG 17 adds the WAL summarizer (for incremental backup) and PG 18 adds I/O workers (<code>io_method = worker</code>).</p><p>Processes share data through shared memory (shared buffers, WAL buffers, lock table, CLOG cache). The model is robust (a crashing backend cannot corrupt another's private memory) but each connection costs several MB and fork time — hence connection poolers.</p>`,
  tags: ['architecture','process-model'] },
{ q: `Why are thousands of direct connections bad for PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>Each connection is an OS process with its own memory (catalog caches, plan caches, <code>work_mem</code> allocations), typically 5–10 MB+ when active. Beyond that, many connections hurt even when idle: snapshot acquisition historically scaled with <code>max_connections</code> (much improved in PG 14), lock manager and <code>ProcArray</code> contention rise, and CPU context switching explodes when hundreds are active on, say, 16 cores. Throughput usually peaks around 2–4× CPU cores of <em>active</em> connections.</p><p>The fix is a pooler such as <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer in transaction mode, or application-side pools sized small.</p>`,
  tags: ['connections','pooling'] },
{ q: `What is shared_buffers and how do you size it?`,
  a: `<p><code>shared_buffers</code> is PostgreSQL's own page cache in shared memory (default 128 MB). All reads and writes go through it. PostgreSQL also relies on the OS page cache (double buffering), so the common starting point is <strong>~25% of RAM</strong> on a dedicated server, rarely above 40%. Requires a restart to change.</p><p>Validate with <span class="tag ext">extension</span> <code>pg_buffercache</code> and the cache hit ratio in <code>pg_stat_database</code>/<code>pg_statio_user_tables</code>. On Linux, enable huge pages (<code>huge_pages = try</code>/<code>on</code>) for large settings to reduce page-table overhead.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','gucs'] },
{ q: `What does effective_cache_size do?`,
  a: `<p>It allocates nothing. It is a <strong>planner hint</strong> estimating how much memory (shared buffers + OS cache) is available for caching a query's data. Higher values make index scans look cheaper because repeated page fetches are assumed cached. Default 4 GB; usually set to 50–75% of RAM.</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','gucs','memory'] },
{ q: `Explain work_mem and why it is dangerous to set high globally.`,
  a: `<p><code>work_mem</code> (default 4 MB) is the memory each <strong>sort or hash operation</strong> may use before spilling to temporary files. A single query can have many such nodes, and parallel workers each get their own; hash nodes may use <code>work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier</code> (default 2.0). So 200 connections × 5 nodes × 256 MB could request hundreds of GB and trigger the OOM killer.</p><p>Keep the global value modest and raise it per role or session for reporting: <code>ALTER ROLE reporting SET work_mem = '256MB';</code> or <code>SET LOCAL work_mem</code> in a transaction. Detect spills via <code>log_temp_files = 0</code> and <code>temp_blks_written</code> in <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_stat_statements.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','gucs','work_mem'] },
{ q: `What is maintenance_work_mem used for?`,
  a: `<p>Memory for maintenance operations: <code>VACUUM</code> (storing dead tuple IDs), <code>CREATE INDEX</code>, <code>ALTER TABLE ADD FOREIGN KEY</code>, and <code>REINDEX</code>. Default 64 MB; 1–2 GB is common on large servers because only a few such operations run concurrently. Autovacuum workers use <code>autovacuum_work_mem</code> (-1 means inherit). Before PG 17, vacuum could use at most 1 GB for dead TIDs regardless of the setting; PG 17's new TID store removed that cap and uses far less memory, so vacuums of huge tables need fewer index passes.</p>`,
  tags: ['memory','gucs','vacuum'] },
{ q: `How do you change a configuration parameter, and how do you know whether it needs a restart?`,
  a: `<p>Options, from widest to narrowest scope: edit <code>postgresql.conf</code>; <code>ALTER SYSTEM SET name = value</code> (writes <code>postgresql.auto.conf</code>); <code>ALTER DATABASE ... SET</code>; <code>ALTER ROLE ... SET</code>; <code>SET</code> / <code>SET LOCAL</code> in a session. Then <code>SELECT pg_reload_conf();</code> for reloadable settings.</p><pre>SELECT name, setting, unit, context, source, pending_restart
FROM pg_settings WHERE name IN ('shared_buffers','work_mem');</pre><p><code>context</code> tells you: <code>postmaster</code> = restart required; <code>sighup</code> = reload; <code>superuser</code>/<code>user</code> = settable per session. <code>pending_restart = true</code> flags changed-but-not-applied values.</p>`,
  tags: ['gucs','configuration'] },
{ q: `What is the pg_settings view and pg_file_settings?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_settings</code> shows the current effective value of each GUC, its source (default, configuration file, database, user, session), min/max, context and boot value. <code>pg_file_settings</code> shows what the config files <em>contain</em> and whether each line was applied or has an <code>error</code> — useful for debugging a typo that was silently ignored after a reload. <code>pg_hba_file_rules</code> and <code>pg_ident_file_mappings</code> do the same for authentication files.</p>`,
  tags: ['gucs','catalogs'] },
{ q: `What is the system catalog, and what are pg_class, pg_attribute and pg_namespace?`,
  a: `<p>The catalog is a set of ordinary tables in schema <code>pg_catalog</code> describing every object. <code>pg_class</code> has one row per relation (table <code>r</code>, index <code>i</code>, sequence <code>S</code>, view <code>v</code>, materialized view <code>m</code>, partitioned table <code>p</code>, partitioned index <code>I</code>, TOAST <code>t</code>, foreign table <code>f</code>), with <code>relpages</code>, <code>reltuples</code>, <code>relfilenode</code>, <code>relfrozenxid</code>. <code>pg_attribute</code> has one row per column; <code>pg_namespace</code> per schema. <code>information_schema</code> is the portable <span class="tag std">SQL standard</span> view layer on top, slower and less complete.</p>`,
  tags: ['catalogs'] },
{ q: `What are OID-alias types like regclass and why are they handy?`,
  a: `<p>Types such as <code>regclass</code>, <code>regproc</code>, <code>regtype</code>, <code>regrole</code> are OIDs that print as names and parse names into OIDs using the current <code>search_path</code>:</p><pre>SELECT 'orders'::regclass::oid;
SELECT relname FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'public.orders'::regclass;
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('orders'));</pre><p>They save joins to <code>pg_class</code>/<code>pg_namespace</code> and raise an error if the object does not exist (<code>to_regclass()</code> returns NULL instead).</p>`,
  tags: ['catalogs'] },
{ q: `How do you find a table's size including indexes and TOAST?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('orders'))        AS heap_main_fork,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size('orders'))           AS heap_plus_toast_fsm_vm,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size('orders'))         AS indexes,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('orders'))  AS everything;</pre><p><code>pg_relation_size</code> is a single fork; <code>pg_table_size</code> adds TOAST, FSM and VM; <code>pg_total_relation_size</code> adds all indexes. <code>pg_database_size()</code> covers a whole database.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','catalogs'] },
{ q: `What are relation forks?`,
  a: `<p>Each relation is stored as several files ("forks") under <code>base/&lt;dboid&gt;/&lt;relfilenode&gt;</code>: the <strong>main</strong> fork (data), <code>_fsm</code> (Free Space Map — free bytes per page, used by INSERT to find room), <code>_vm</code> (Visibility Map — 2 bits per page: all-visible, all-frozen), and <code>_init</code> for unlogged relations (an empty copy used to reset them after a crash). Files are split into 1 GB segments (<code>.1</code>, <code>.2</code>, ...).</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','internals'] },
{ q: `What is the Visibility Map used for?`,
  a: `<p>Two bits per heap page: <strong>all-visible</strong> (every tuple visible to all transactions) and <strong>all-frozen</strong>. It enables <strong>index-only scans</strong> (skip the heap fetch for all-visible pages — check <code>Heap Fetches</code> in EXPLAIN ANALYZE), lets VACUUM skip all-visible pages, and lets aggressive (freeze) vacuums skip all-frozen pages. Only VACUUM sets the bits; any modification clears them. So an index-only scan on a heavily updated table that is rarely vacuumed degrades into a normal index scan.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','vacuum','indexes'] },
{ q: `What is a HOT update?`,
  a: `<p>A Heap-Only Tuple update happens when (1) no indexed column changes and (2) the new tuple fits on the <strong>same page</strong>. Then no new index entries are created: the old tuple points to the new one via a chain, and indexes keep pointing at the original line pointer. Dead HOT tuples can be pruned during normal page access without VACUUM.</p><p>Encourage HOT by not indexing frequently-updated columns and leaving free space with <code>fillfactor</code> (e.g. 80–90 on update-heavy tables). Monitor with <code>n_tup_hot_upd</code> vs <code>n_tup_upd</code> in <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code>. PG 16 also allows HOT when only BRIN-indexed columns change.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','hot','performance'] },
{ q: `What does fillfactor do?`,
  a: `<p>A storage parameter (percentage, 10–100) that tells INSERTs to leave pages partly empty. Tables default to 100; B-tree indexes to 90. For update-heavy tables, a table fillfactor of 70–90 reserves room so updates can stay on the same page (HOT), reducing index churn and bloat:</p><pre>ALTER TABLE accounts SET (fillfactor = 85);</pre><p>It only affects newly written pages; existing data needs a rewrite (<code>VACUUM FULL</code> or pg_repack) to adopt it. Do not use it on append-only tables — it simply wastes space.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','hot'] },
{ q: `Which transaction isolation levels does PostgreSQL support?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Read Committed</strong> (default): each statement sees a fresh snapshot. Read Uncommitted is accepted but behaves as Read Committed — PostgreSQL never shows dirty reads.</li><li><strong>Repeatable Read</strong>: one snapshot for the whole transaction (true snapshot isolation; also prevents phantoms). Concurrent updates to the same row fail with <code>could not serialize access due to concurrent update</code>.</li><li><strong>Serializable</strong>: Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) — tracks read/write dependencies with predicate (SIRead) locks and aborts one transaction of a dangerous structure, preventing write skew. Clients must retry on SQLSTATE <code>40001</code>.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['transactions','isolation'] },
{ q: `What is write skew, and which isolation level prevents it?`,
  a: `<p>Two transactions read overlapping data, make decisions based on it, and write <em>different</em> rows, together violating an invariant. Classic example: two on-call doctors each check "is another doctor on call?", see yes, and both go off call. Under Repeatable Read (snapshot isolation) both commit. Only <strong>Serializable</strong> (SSI) detects the rw-dependency cycle and aborts one. Alternatives: explicit <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> on the rows that represent the invariant, or a constraint.</p>`,
  tags: ['transactions','isolation'] },
{ q: `What row-level lock modes exist?`,
  a: `<p>From strongest to weakest: <code>FOR UPDATE</code> (delete or update any column), <code>FOR NO KEY UPDATE</code> (taken by ordinary UPDATEs that do not change key columns), <code>FOR SHARE</code>, <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code> (taken by FK checks; conflicts only with FOR UPDATE and key changes). Row locks are stored in the tuple's <code>xmax</code>, not in the shared lock table, so locking millions of rows does not exhaust memory. Modifiers: <code>NOWAIT</code> errors immediately; <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> skips locked rows — the basis of queue tables.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','transactions'] },
{ q: `Name the table-level lock modes and which common commands take them.`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>ACCESS SHARE</code>: SELECT.</li><li><code>ROW SHARE</code>: SELECT FOR UPDATE/SHARE.</li><li><code>ROW EXCLUSIVE</code>: INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE.</li><li><code>SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE</code>: VACUUM, ANALYZE, CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY, VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, ATTACH PARTITION (on parent).</li><li><code>SHARE</code>: CREATE INDEX (non-concurrent) — blocks writes.</li><li><code>SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE</code>: CREATE TRIGGER, adding an FK (on both tables).</li><li><code>EXCLUSIVE</code>: REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY.</li><li><code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code>: DROP, TRUNCATE, most ALTER TABLE, VACUUM FULL, CLUSTER, REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW — blocks even SELECT.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['locks','ddl'] },
{ q: `Why can a quick ALTER TABLE take the whole application down?`,
  a: `<p>Lock queueing. The <code>ALTER TABLE</code> needs <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code>. If a long-running SELECT holds <code>ACCESS SHARE</code>, the ALTER waits — and every new query on that table queues <em>behind</em> the ALTER because lock requests are granted in order. The ALTER itself is instant, but the queue makes the table unavailable for as long as the old query runs.</p><p>Mitigation: always <code>SET lock_timeout = '3s'</code> before DDL and retry in a loop; terminate or wait out long transactions first; use concurrent variants (<code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>, <code>NOT VALID</code> + <code>VALIDATE</code>).</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','ddl','migrations'] },
{ q: `What are advisory locks?`,
  a: `<p>Application-defined locks on a 64-bit key (or two 32-bit keys) that PostgreSQL tracks but never takes automatically. Session-level (<code>pg_advisory_lock</code>, released by <code>pg_advisory_unlock</code> or disconnect) and transaction-level (<code>pg_advisory_xact_lock</code>, released at commit/rollback); <code>pg_try_advisory_lock</code> returns false instead of waiting. Uses: ensuring a cron job runs once across many app servers, migration tools (Flyway, Liquibase) serializing runs. Caveat: session-level advisory locks behave badly behind PgBouncer transaction pooling, because the session is not yours.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','concurrency'] },
{ q: `How does PostgreSQL detect deadlocks?`,
  a: `<p>When a backend has waited for a lock longer than <code>deadlock_timeout</code> (default 1 s), it builds the wait-for graph; if it finds a cycle, it aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE <code>40P01</code> (deadlock detected) and logs the processes and queries involved. The 1 s delay keeps the expensive check off the fast path. <code>log_lock_waits = on</code> logs any wait exceeding <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, which is invaluable for diagnosing contention.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','deadlocks'] },
{ q: `What is pg_stat_activity and what are the key columns?`,
  a: `<p>One row per server process. Key columns: <code>pid</code>, <code>usename</code>, <code>datname</code>, <code>application_name</code>, <code>client_addr</code>, <code>backend_type</code>, <code>state</code> (active, idle, idle in transaction, idle in transaction (aborted)), <code>xact_start</code>, <code>query_start</code>, <code>state_change</code>, <code>wait_event_type</code>/<code>wait_event</code>, <code>backend_xid</code>, <code>backend_xmin</code>, <code>query</code>, <code>query_id</code> (with <code>compute_query_id</code>).</p><pre>SELECT pid, state, now()-xact_start AS xact_age, wait_event_type, wait_event, left(query,80)
FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; 'idle' ORDER BY xact_start;</pre>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','pg_stat'] },
{ q: `What do wait_event_type values tell you?`,
  a: `<p>They show what an active backend is blocked on: <code>Lock</code> (heavyweight lock — table/row/tuple/transactionid), <code>LWLock</code> (internal lightweight locks like <code>WALWrite</code>, <code>BufferMapping</code>, <code>LockManager</code>), <code>IO</code> (<code>DataFileRead</code>, <code>WALSync</code>), <code>Client</code> (<code>ClientRead</code> — waiting for the app), <code>IPC</code>, <code>Timeout</code>, <code>BufferPin</code>, <code>Activity</code>, <code>Extension</code>. NULL with state active means on CPU. Sampling these every second gives you a poor-man's "active session history".</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','wait-events'] },
{ q: `What is pg_stat_statements?`,
  a: `<p>An <span class="tag ext">extension</span> shipped in contrib that aggregates statistics per normalized query (constants replaced by <code>$1</code>-style placeholders): <code>calls</code>, <code>total_exec_time</code>, <code>mean_exec_time</code>, <code>rows</code>, <code>shared_blks_hit/read</code>, <code>temp_blks_written</code>, <code>wal_bytes</code>, planning time, and JIT stats. Requires <code>shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_stat_statements'</code>, a restart, then <code>CREATE EXTENSION pg_stat_statements;</code>. Sort by <code>total_exec_time</code> to find what costs the server most — usually not the slowest single query but a fast one called millions of times. PG 17 added <code>stats_since</code> and <code>minmax_stats_since</code> for resets.</p>`,
  tags: ['extensions','monitoring','pg_stat_statements'] },
{ q: `What does pg_stat_user_tables tell you?`,
  a: `<p>Per-table counters since the last stats reset: <code>seq_scan</code>/<code>seq_tup_read</code>, <code>idx_scan</code>, <code>n_tup_ins/upd/del/hot_upd</code>, <code>n_live_tup</code>, <code>n_dead_tup</code>, <code>n_mod_since_analyze</code>, <code>n_ins_since_vacuum</code>, <code>last_vacuum</code>, <code>last_autovacuum</code>, <code>last_analyze</code>, <code>last_autoanalyze</code> and vacuum counts. PG 16 added <code>last_seq_scan</code>/<code>last_idx_scan</code>; PG 18 added cumulative vacuum/analyze time columns. Use it to find tables with large sequential scans, high dead-tuple ratios, or tables autovacuum never reaches.</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','pg_stat','vacuum'] },
{ q: `What is pg_stat_io?`,
  a: `<p>A view added in PG 16 that breaks down I/O by <code>backend_type</code>, <code>object</code> (relation, temp relation; WAL added in PG 18) and <code>context</code> (normal, vacuum, bulkread, bulkwrite): reads, writes, extends, hits, evictions, reuses, fsyncs, and timing when <code>track_io_timing</code> is on. It answers questions like "are client backends doing their own buffer writes because the background writer cannot keep up?" PG 18 adds byte counts (<code>read_bytes</code> etc.).</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','pg_stat','io'] },
{ q: `What changed with pg_stat_bgwriter in PostgreSQL 17?`,
  a: `<p>Checkpoint-related columns (<code>checkpoints_timed</code>, <code>checkpoints_req</code>, <code>buffers_checkpoint</code>, write/sync time) moved to the new <code>pg_stat_checkpointer</code> view (as <code>num_timed</code>, <code>num_requested</code>, <code>buffers_written</code>, etc.), and <code>buffers_backend</code>/<code>buffers_backend_fsync</code> were removed in favour of <code>pg_stat_io</code>. <code>pg_stat_bgwriter</code> now only covers the background writer (<code>buffers_clean</code>, <code>maxwritten_clean</code>, <code>buffers_alloc</code>). Monitoring queries written for PG 16 and older must be updated.</p>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','checkpoint','versions'] },
{ q: `What is a replication slot?`,
  a: `<p>A server-side object that remembers how far a consumer (physical standby or logical subscriber) has received WAL, so the primary <strong>retains WAL</strong> (and, for logical slots, catalog rows; with <code>hot_standby_feedback</code>, the standby's xmin) until the consumer confirms it. This prevents a standby from falling behind the WAL that was already recycled.</p><p>The danger: an abandoned or lagging slot retains WAL indefinitely and can fill the disk and block vacuum. Cap it with <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> (PG 13+) — then <code>wal_status</code> becomes <code>lost</code> and the slot is invalidated instead of the primary crashing. PG 18 adds <code>idle_replication_slot_timeout</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','slots','wal'] },
{ q: `How do you inspect replication slots?`,
  a: `<pre>SELECT slot_name, slot_type, active, wal_status, safe_wal_size,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained,
       inactive_since, invalidation_reason
FROM pg_replication_slots;</pre><p><code>wal_status</code>: <code>reserved</code>, <code>extended</code> (beyond max_wal_size but within limits), <code>unreserved</code> (about to be lost), <code>lost</code>. <code>inactive_since</code> and <code>invalidation_reason</code> were added in PG 17; <code>conflicting</code> in PG 16 (logical slots on standbys). Drop an unused slot with <code>SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('name');</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','slots','monitoring'] },
{ q: `Physical (streaming) vs logical replication in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Physical streaming replication</strong> ships raw WAL bytes; the standby is a block-for-block copy of the whole cluster, read-only (hot standby), same major version and architecture. Ideal for HA and read scaling.</p><p><strong>Logical replication</strong> (<code>CREATE PUBLICATION</code> / <code>CREATE SUBSCRIPTION</code>) decodes WAL into row changes per table. Subscribers are writable, can be a different major version, can replicate a subset of tables, rows (row filters, PG 15+) and columns. Limitations: no DDL replication, sequence values are not replicated (as of PG 18 you must copy them with <code>setval()</code> at cutover), tables need a primary key or <code>REPLICA IDENTITY</code>, large objects not replicated. Ideal for major upgrades with minimal downtime, consolidation and CDC.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','logical'] },
{ q: `What is REPLICA IDENTITY?`,
  a: `<p>It defines which old-row values are written to WAL for <code>UPDATE</code>/<code>DELETE</code> so logical replication can find the row on the subscriber: <code>DEFAULT</code> (primary key), <code>USING INDEX idx</code> (a unique, non-partial, non-deferrable index on NOT NULL columns), <code>FULL</code> (entire old row — high WAL cost and full-table scans on the subscriber unless a usable index exists; PG 16 can use non-unique B-tree indexes there), or <code>NOTHING</code>. A table without a PK published for updates/deletes will make those statements fail on the publisher with an error.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','logical'] },
{ q: `What is logical decoding and what uses it?`,
  a: `<p>The infrastructure that reads WAL from a logical replication slot and converts it into a stream of committed changes via an <strong>output plugin</strong>: <code>pgoutput</code> (built in, used by native logical replication), <code>test_decoding</code> (contrib example), or third-party plugins such as <code>wal2json</code>. Consumers include Debezium <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> for CDC into Kafka, and online migration tools. PG 16 allows logical decoding on standbys, and PG 17 adds failover slots so subscribers can continue after a primary failover.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','logical','cdc'] },
{ q: `What are failover slots in PostgreSQL 17?`,
  a: `<p>Before PG 17, logical replication slots existed only on the primary; after a failover the new primary had no slot, and subscribers (or Debezium) had to resync. PG 17 lets a logical slot be created with <code>failover = true</code> (or a subscription with <code>failover = true</code>), and a physical standby with <code>sync_replication_slots = on</code> (plus <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> and a physical slot listed in <code>synchronized_standby_slots</code> on the primary) keeps synchronized copies. After promotion, logical consumers can continue from the new primary.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','logical','ha','versions'] },
{ q: `How does pg_upgrade work?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_upgrade</code> upgrades a cluster to a new major version without dump/reload of data: it dumps and restores only the <strong>schema</strong> (catalog) into a freshly <code>initdb</code>'d new cluster, then transfers data files, which is possible because the on-disk heap/index format is stable across majors. Transfer modes: <code>--copy</code> (default, safe, slow), <code>--link</code> (hard links; minutes for TB, but the old cluster is unusable once the new one starts), <code>--clone</code> (reflink copy-on-write on supporting filesystems), <code>--copy-file-range</code> (PG 17), and <code>--swap</code> (PG 18, moves directories — fastest).</p><p>Always run <code>pg_upgrade --check</code> first. Since PG 18, optimizer statistics are preserved; before that, run <code>vacuumdb --all --analyze-in-stages</code> immediately afterwards or queries will be slow. Extensions must be installed for the new version.</p>`,
  tags: ['upgrades','pg_upgrade'] },
{ q: `What options exist for a major-version upgrade and how do you choose?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>pg_dump/pg_restore</strong>: simplest, works across anything, but downtime proportional to data size — fine for tens of GB.</li><li><strong>pg_upgrade --link</strong>: minutes of downtime regardless of size; in-place; rollback needs a backup or a standby kept on the old version.</li><li><strong>Logical replication</strong>: build a new-version cluster, subscribe, let it catch up, sync sequences, switch traffic — downtime of seconds; more moving parts (no DDL replication, PK required). <code>pg_createsubscriber</code> (PG 17) converts a physical standby into a logical subscriber to avoid the initial copy.</li></ul><p>Minor versions (e.g. 17.4 → 17.6) are just binary swaps plus restart.</p>`,
  tags: ['upgrades','replication'] },
{ q: `What are extensions and how are they managed?`,
  a: `<p>Packages of SQL objects (types, functions, operators, index methods) plus optional shared libraries, installed with <code>CREATE EXTENSION name</code> into a database. Files must exist on the server (<code>.control</code> + SQL scripts + <code>.so</code>). Catalog: <code>pg_extension</code> (installed), <code>pg_available_extensions</code> (installable with default and installed versions). Upgrade with <code>ALTER EXTENSION name UPDATE</code>. Some need <code>shared_preload_libraries</code> (pg_stat_statements, auto_explain, pg_cron, Citus, TimescaleDB). Many are "trusted" (PG 13+) so non-superusers with CREATE on the database may install them. Examples: contrib modules (pg_trgm, pgcrypto, postgres_fdw) and external projects (pgvector, PostGIS, Citus).</p>`,
  tags: ['extensions'] },
{ q: `What is pgvector, and is it part of core PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgvector is an open-source extension, <strong>not core</strong>. It adds the <code>vector</code> type (plus <code>halfvec</code>, <code>sparsevec</code>, <code>bit</code> usage), distance operators <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> (L2), <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (cosine distance), <code>&lt;#&gt;</code> (negative inner product), <code>&lt;+&gt;</code> (L1), and approximate nearest-neighbour indexes <strong>HNSW</strong> and <strong>IVFFlat</strong>. It lets you store embeddings next to relational data and filter with SQL, which is attractive for RAG systems. It is available on most managed services (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure, Supabase, Neon).</p>`,
  tags: ['extensions','pgvector','ai'] },
{ q: `What does pg_trgm provide?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> (contrib) pg_trgm splits text into trigrams (3-character sequences) and provides <code>similarity()</code>, the <code>%</code> similarity operator, <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> distance, and GIN/GiST operator classes (<code>gin_trgm_ops</code>, <code>gist_trgm_ops</code>). Crucially, a trigram GIN index accelerates <code>LIKE '%foo%'</code>, <code>ILIKE</code> and regex searches that B-tree cannot, and fuzzy matching for typos:</p><pre>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX ON customers USING gin (name gin_trgm_ops);
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE name ILIKE '%sharma%';</pre>`,
  tags: ['extensions','pg_trgm','search'] },
{ q: `What is postgres_fdw?`,
  a: `<p>A contrib <span class="tag ext">extension</span> implementing SQL/MED foreign data wrappers for remote PostgreSQL servers: <code>CREATE SERVER</code>, <code>CREATE USER MAPPING</code>, then <code>IMPORT FOREIGN SCHEMA</code> or <code>CREATE FOREIGN TABLE</code>. It pushes down WHERE clauses, joins between tables on the same server, aggregates, sorts and LIMIT when safe; supports writes and (PG 14+) async execution for partitions that are foreign tables. Uses: cross-database queries, simple sharding experiments, migrations. Watch for row-by-row fetches when pushdown fails — check <code>EXPLAIN VERBOSE</code> for the Remote SQL.</p>`,
  tags: ['extensions','fdw'] },
{ q: `How are sequences and identity columns implemented, and why do they have gaps?`,
  a: `<p>A sequence is a special single-row relation; <code>nextval()</code> is non-transactional — a value obtained in a rolled-back transaction is never returned, so gaps are normal. Sequences also pre-allocate with <code>CACHE</code> per session and log ahead 32 values to WAL, so a crash can skip values. <code>GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY</code> (<span class="tag std">SQL standard</span>) is the modern replacement for <code>serial</code>: the sequence is owned by the column, permissions follow the table, and <code>ALWAYS</code> rejects manual values unless <code>OVERRIDING SYSTEM VALUE</code>. Never use sequences where gapless numbering is legally required (invoice numbers) — use a counter row updated in the same transaction.</p>`,
  tags: ['sequences','identity'] },
{ q: `What is uuidv7() and why does it matter?`,
  a: `<p>PG 18 added a core <code>uuidv7()</code> function (and <code>uuidv4()</code> as an alias of <code>gen_random_uuid()</code>). UUIDv7 embeds a Unix millisecond timestamp in the high bits, so values are <strong>roughly time-ordered</strong>. Random v4 UUIDs insert all over a B-tree index, causing page splits, poor cache locality and large WAL (full-page images); v7 values append near the right edge like a bigint sequence while remaining globally unique and generatable by clients. Caveat: the timestamp leaks creation time.</p>`,
  tags: ['types','uuid','versions'] },
{ q: `What are the main index access methods in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>B-tree</strong>: default; equality, range, sorting, <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> (with C collation or <code>text_pattern_ops</code>).</li><li><strong>Hash</strong>: equality only; WAL-logged since PG 10; rarely better than B-tree.</li><li><strong>GIN</strong>: inverted index for multi-valued data — arrays, jsonb, full-text tsvector, trigrams.</li><li><strong>GiST</strong>: balanced framework for geometric, range, nearest-neighbour, exclusion constraints, PostGIS.</li><li><strong>SP-GiST</strong>: space-partitioned trees (quad-trees, radix trees) — IP ranges, points, text prefixes.</li><li><strong>BRIN</strong>: block range summaries — tiny indexes for huge naturally ordered tables (time series).</li></ul><p>Extensions add more: <span class="tag ext">extension</span> bloom, and pgvector's hnsw and ivfflat.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes'] },
{ q: `What is B-tree deduplication and bottom-up deletion?`,
  a: `<p>Since PG 13, B-tree leaf pages store duplicate keys once with a sorted posting list of TIDs, shrinking indexes on low-cardinality columns by up to several times (<code>deduplicate_items</code> storage parameter, on by default). PG 14 added <strong>bottom-up index deletion</strong>: when a page would split because of version churn from non-HOT updates, the index first deletes entries pointing to dead tuples, avoiding "version-driven" page splits and limiting index bloat between vacuums.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','btree','internals'] },
{ q: `What is B-tree skip scan in PostgreSQL 18?`,
  a: `<p>Previously, a multicolumn B-tree on <code>(a, b)</code> was only efficient when the query constrained the leading column <code>a</code>. PG 18 can perform a <strong>skip scan</strong>: when <code>a</code> has few distinct values and the query filters only on <code>b</code>, the scan internally iterates over each distinct <code>a</code> and probes <code>b</code> within it. This reduces the need for duplicate indexes like a separate one on <code>(b)</code>, but only helps when the leading column has low cardinality.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','btree','versions'] },
{ q: `What is asynchronous I/O in PostgreSQL 18?`,
  a: `<p>PG 18 introduced an AIO subsystem controlled by <code>io_method</code>: <code>worker</code> (default — dedicated I/O worker processes, count set by <code>io_workers</code>), <code>io_uring</code> (Linux, if built with liburing), or <code>sync</code> (old behaviour). Initially used for sequential scans, bitmap heap scans and VACUUM reads, allowing many reads in flight instead of one-at-a-time synchronous <code>pread</code> calls. It matters most on high-latency cloud storage (EBS, Persistent Disk), where read throughput can improve markedly. <code>effective_io_concurrency</code> and <code>maintenance_io_concurrency</code> control depth (both now default 16).</p>`,
  tags: ['io','versions','performance'] },
{ q: `What are data checksums?`,
  a: `<p>A per-page checksum written when a page is flushed and verified when it is read, detecting silent storage corruption (bit rot, bad controllers). Enabled at <code>initdb --data-checksums</code> or offline with <code>pg_checksums --enable</code>. PG 18 makes checksums the <strong>default</strong> for new clusters (<code>--no-data-checksums</code> to disable) — note <code>pg_upgrade</code> requires both clusters to match, so upgrading an old non-checksummed cluster needs <code>--no-data-checksums</code> or enabling them first. Check with <code>SHOW data_checksums;</code> and failures in <code>pg_stat_database.checksum_failures</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','durability','versions'] },
{ q: `What is pg_hba.conf and how is it evaluated?`,
  a: `<p>Host-Based Authentication: each line is <code>TYPE DATABASE USER ADDRESS METHOD [options]</code>, e.g. <code>hostssl app_db app_user 10.0.0.0/16 scram-sha-256</code>. Lines are read <strong>top to bottom and the first match wins</strong> — there is no fall-through if authentication fails. Types: <code>local</code> (Unix socket), <code>host</code>, <code>hostssl</code>, <code>hostnossl</code>, <code>hostgssenc</code>. Methods: <code>scram-sha-256</code> (recommended), <code>md5</code> (deprecated in PG 18), <code>peer</code>, <code>cert</code>, <code>ldap</code>, <code>gss</code>, <code>oauth</code> (PG 18), <code>reject</code>, <code>trust</code> (never on networks). Reload to apply; inspect with <code>pg_hba_file_rules</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['security','authentication'] },
{ q: `Roles, users and groups in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>PostgreSQL has only <strong>roles</strong>. A "user" is a role with <code>LOGIN</code>; a "group" is a role others are granted membership in. Membership can be with <code>INHERIT</code> (privileges apply automatically) or require <code>SET ROLE</code>. Predefined roles grant common capabilities without superuser: <code>pg_read_all_data</code>, <code>pg_write_all_data</code> (14+), <code>pg_monitor</code>, <code>pg_signal_backend</code>, <code>pg_maintain</code> (17 — VACUUM/ANALYZE/REINDEX/REFRESH on all tables), <code>pg_checkpoint</code>, <code>pg_create_subscription</code>. PG 16 tightened <code>CREATEROLE</code>: such roles only get admin over roles they create.</p>`,
  tags: ['security','roles'] },
{ q: `What changed about the public schema in PostgreSQL 15?`,
  a: `<p>From PG 15, the <code>CREATE</code> privilege on the <code>public</code> schema is no longer granted to <code>PUBLIC</code> in new databases, and the schema is owned by <code>pg_database_owner</code>. This closes a classic attack where any user could create functions or tables in <code>public</code> that shadow objects for other users via <code>search_path</code>. Upgraded databases keep the old permissions, so run <code>REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC;</code> explicitly.</p>`,
  tags: ['security','schemas','versions'] },
{ q: `What is row-level security (RLS)?`,
  a: `<p>A core feature that adds per-row predicates to every query on a table: <code>ALTER TABLE users ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;</code> then <code>CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON users USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::int);</code>. <code>USING</code> filters visible rows; <code>WITH CHECK</code> validates new rows. Table owners and superusers bypass RLS unless <code>FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY</code> and they are not superuser/<code>BYPASSRLS</code>. Policies are permissive (OR-ed) by default or <code>AS RESTRICTIVE</code> (AND-ed). Watch performance: index the tenant column and keep policy functions <code>STABLE</code>/leakproof.</p>`,
  tags: ['security','rls','multi-tenant'] },
{ q: `What is a tablespace, and when would you use one?`,
  a: `<p>A tablespace maps a name to a filesystem directory (<code>CREATE TABLESPACE fast LOCATION '/nvme/pg';</code>), letting you place tables or indexes on different storage. Historic uses: hot indexes on SSD, archive data on cheap disk. In modern cloud deployments they are rarely worthwhile: they complicate backups and replicas (paths must exist on standbys), and a single fast volume is simpler. <code>temp_tablespaces</code> is still occasionally useful to direct temp-file spills to local NVMe.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage'] },
{ q: `What are unlogged tables?`,
  a: `<p><code>CREATE UNLOGGED TABLE</code> skips WAL for its data, making writes much faster, but the table is <strong>truncated after a crash</strong> and is not replicated (it appears empty on standbys). Good for staging/ETL scratch data, caches, and rebuildable session data. <code>ALTER TABLE ... SET LOGGED</code> converts it (rewriting and WAL-logging it all). Temporary tables are also unlogged and session-private.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','wal'] },
{ q: `How does PostgreSQL implement temporary tables, and what are their pitfalls?`,
  a: `<p>Temporary tables live in a per-session <code>pg_temp_N</code> schema, are unlogged, use session-local <code>temp_buffers</code> rather than shared buffers, and vanish at session end (or at commit with <code>ON COMMIT DROP</code>). Pitfalls: each create/drop inserts and deletes catalog rows, so heavy temp-table churn bloats <code>pg_class</code>/<code>pg_attribute</code>; autovacuum never processes them, so you must <code>ANALYZE</code> them manually for good plans; they are invisible to other sessions and incompatible with transaction-mode pooling unless created and dropped within one transaction.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','temp'] },
{ q: `What is a materialized view and how do you refresh it without blocking reads?`,
  a: `<p>A materialized view stores the result of its query physically and can be indexed. <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW mv</code> recomputes it under an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock (readers blocked). <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY mv</code> computes the new result, diffs it against the old and applies the changes, allowing reads throughout; it requires a <strong>unique index</strong> on the materialized view and is slower when most rows change. There is no built-in incremental refresh — options are scheduled refreshes (e.g. via <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_cron), trigger-maintained summary tables, or the third-party pg_ivm extension.</p>`,
  tags: ['views','materialized-views'] },
{ q: `What is the difference between a function and a procedure?`,
  a: `<p>Functions (<code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>) return a value or set and are called inside SQL statements; they run within the caller's transaction and cannot commit. Procedures (<code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code>, PG 11+) are invoked with <code>CALL</code>, return via OUT parameters, and can run <code>COMMIT</code>/<code>ROLLBACK</code> inside their body (when not called within an explicit transaction block) — useful for batched maintenance such as deleting 100M rows in committed chunks.</p>`,
  tags: ['plpgsql','functions'] },
{ q: `What do volatility categories (IMMUTABLE, STABLE, VOLATILE) mean?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>VOLATILE</strong> (default): may change results each call or have side effects (<code>random()</code>, <code>nextval()</code>); evaluated per row, never optimized away.</li><li><strong>STABLE</strong>: same result for the same arguments within one statement (<code>now()</code>, lookups); can be used for index scan comparisons.</li><li><strong>IMMUTABLE</strong>: same result forever for the same arguments; can be constant-folded and used in <strong>expression indexes</strong> and generated columns.</li></ul><p>Mislabelling a function IMMUTABLE when it depends on a table, timezone or <code>search_path</code> leads to silently wrong index contents. Also consider <code>PARALLEL SAFE</code> labelling, or the function will block parallel plans.</p>`,
  tags: ['functions','planner'] },
{ q: `What are generated columns, and what did PostgreSQL 18 add?`,
  a: `<p><code>GENERATED ALWAYS AS (expr) STORED</code> columns (PG 12+) are computed on write and stored on disk; the expression must be immutable. PG 18 adds <strong>VIRTUAL</strong> generated columns, computed at read time and taking no storage — and virtual becomes the default when neither is specified. Virtual columns cannot currently be indexed directly (use an expression index on the same expression) and have some restrictions (e.g. no user-defined types or functions in the expression). Stored ones suit expensive expressions that are read often, like a tsvector for full-text search.</p>`,
  tags: ['ddl','generated-columns','versions'] },
{ q: `What is MERGE and what did PostgreSQL 17 add to it?`,
  a: `<p><code>MERGE</code> (<span class="tag std">SQL standard</span>, PG 15+) applies conditional INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a source to a target in one statement with <code>WHEN MATCHED</code> / <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED</code> clauses. PG 17 added <code>RETURNING</code> (with <code>merge_action()</code> to tell which action happened), <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE</code> (act on target rows missing from the source), and updatable views as targets. Note: unlike <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT</code>, MERGE is not race-free against concurrent inserts — two sessions can both decide "not matched" and one gets a unique violation. For idempotent upserts under concurrency, prefer ON CONFLICT.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','merge','versions'] },
{ q: `How does INSERT ... ON CONFLICT work under concurrency?`,
  a: `<p>It uses speculative insertion: it inserts a tuple, checks the arbiter unique index, and if a conflicting row committed concurrently it kills its speculative tuple and takes the conflict path (<code>DO NOTHING</code> or <code>DO UPDATE</code>, which locks and updates the existing row). This guarantees an atomic outcome — insert or update — even in Read Committed, without unique-violation errors. Caveats: a conflict target (unique index or constraint) is required for DO UPDATE; a single statement cannot affect the same row twice (<code>ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE command cannot affect row a second time</code>) — de-duplicate the input; sequence values are consumed even when updating.</p>`,
  tags: ['sql','upsert','concurrency'] },
{ q: `What is SKIP LOCKED and how is it used for job queues?`,
  a: `<pre>WITH job AS (
  SELECT id FROM jobs
  WHERE status = 'queued'
  ORDER BY created_at
  LIMIT 10
  FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)
UPDATE jobs SET status = 'running', started_at = now()
FROM job WHERE jobs.id = job.id
RETURNING jobs.*;</pre><p>Each worker locks a batch of unlocked rows and skips those already claimed, so many workers dequeue concurrently without blocking or double-processing. Add a partial index <code>ON jobs (created_at) WHERE status = 'queued'</code> and vacuum aggressively, because queue tables churn dead tuples rapidly.</p>`,
  tags: ['locks','queues','patterns'] },
{ q: `What are LISTEN and NOTIFY?`,
  a: `<p>A built-in pub/sub mechanism: sessions <code>LISTEN channel</code>; <code>NOTIFY channel, 'payload'</code> (or <code>pg_notify()</code>) delivers to listeners <strong>at commit</strong>, de-duplicating identical notifications in a transaction. Payload limit ~8000 bytes; the queue is 8 GB. Not durable — listeners that are disconnected miss messages. Use it for cache invalidation or waking workers that then read a durable table. Incompatible with PgBouncer transaction pooling for listeners (they need a dedicated session). Historically, NOTIFY takes a global lock at commit, which limits very high-rate use.</p>`,
  tags: ['pubsub','features'] },
{ q: `What is a prepared transaction (two-phase commit) and why is it risky?`,
  a: `<p><code>PREPARE TRANSACTION 'gid'</code> persists a transaction's state so it can be committed later by an external coordinator (<code>COMMIT PREPARED 'gid'</code>), even after a restart. Enabled only if <code>max_prepared_transactions</code> &gt; 0 (default 0). Risk: an orphaned prepared transaction holds its locks and its xmin <strong>forever</strong>, blocking vacuum and pushing toward wraparound, and does not appear as a normal session. Check <code>pg_prepared_xacts</code> and alert on old entries.</p>`,
  tags: ['transactions','2pc'] },
{ q: `What is pg_dump, and what are its formats?`,
  a: `<p>A logical backup tool producing a consistent snapshot of one database (using a Repeatable Read snapshot, so it does not block writes, though it takes <code>ACCESS SHARE</code> locks that conflict with DDL). Formats: plain SQL (<code>-Fp</code>, restore with psql), custom (<code>-Fc</code>, compressed, selective restore via <code>pg_restore</code>), directory (<code>-Fd</code>, supports parallel dump with <code>-j</code>), and tar. <code>pg_dumpall</code> covers roles and tablespaces (globals). It is not PITR-capable and restore requires rebuilding indexes — slow for multi-TB databases, where physical backups are preferred.</p>`,
  tags: ['backup','pg_dump'] },
{ q: `What is pg_basebackup and what is incremental backup in PostgreSQL 17?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_basebackup</code> takes a physical copy of the entire cluster over the replication protocol while it runs, plus the WAL needed to make it consistent. Used to seed standbys and for PITR base backups. PG 17 added <strong>incremental backups</strong>: with <code>summarize_wal = on</code>, the WAL summarizer tracks changed blocks; <code>pg_basebackup --incremental=previous/backup_manifest</code> copies only changed blocks, and <code>pg_combinebackup</code> reconstructs a full data directory at restore time. Dedicated <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>s like pgBackRest and Barman have long provided incremental/differential backups, parallelism, compression, encryption and retention management.</p>`,
  tags: ['backup','pitr','versions'] },
{ q: `How does point-in-time recovery (PITR) work?`,
  a: `<p>You need a physical base backup plus continuous WAL archiving (<code>archive_mode = on</code>, <code>archive_command</code> or <code>archive_library</code>). To recover: restore the base backup, create <code>recovery.signal</code>, set <code>restore_command</code> and a target such as <code>recovery_target_time = '2025-10-01 14:29:00+05:30'</code> (or <code>_lsn</code>, <code>_xid</code>, <code>_name</code>), and <code>recovery_target_action = 'promote'</code> or <code>'pause'</code>. The server replays archived WAL up to the target. Pausing lets you inspect data before promoting. Practise restores regularly — untested backups are hopes, not backups.</p>`,
  tags: ['backup','pitr','wal'] },
{ q: `What are timelines?`,
  a: `<p>Each time a cluster is promoted or finishes PITR, it starts a new <strong>timeline</strong> (ID increments), and WAL segment names include it (<code>000000020000000A000000F3</code>). A <code>.history</code> file records where it branched. Timelines prevent WAL from different histories after a recovery from overwriting each other, and let you recover along a specific branch (<code>recovery_target_timeline = 'latest'</code>, the default since PG 12). Old primaries that diverged need <code>pg_rewind</code> or a fresh base backup to follow the new timeline.</p>`,
  tags: ['wal','pitr','replication'] },
{ q: `What is pg_rewind?`,
  a: `<p>A core tool that resynchronizes a former primary with the new primary after a failover by copying only blocks that changed since the timelines diverged, instead of a full base backup. Requirements: the old server shut down cleanly, and either <code>wal_log_hints = on</code> or data checksums enabled, and the WAL since divergence must be available. HA managers such as <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> Patroni use it automatically to re-join the old primary as a standby.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','ha','tools'] },
{ q: `What is hot_standby_feedback and what is the trade-off?`,
  a: `<p>When on, a standby reports its oldest snapshot xmin to the primary, so the primary's vacuum does not remove rows that standby queries still need — avoiding the error <code>canceling statement due to conflict with recovery</code>. The trade-off: long queries on the replica now cause <strong>bloat on the primary</strong> and delay freezing. Alternatives: <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> (default 30 s; let replay wait for queries, increasing replica lag), or dedicated reporting replicas with a large delay setting, plus <code>statement_timeout</code> on replicas.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','vacuum','standby'] },
{ q: `What is a recovery conflict on a hot standby?`,
  a: `<p>A standby's WAL replay sometimes needs to do something incompatible with a running query: remove row versions that the query's snapshot can still see (vacuum cleanup), take an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock replayed from the primary (DROP, TRUNCATE, lock on table), drop a tablespace or database, or handle a buffer pin. Replay waits up to <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> / <code>max_standby_archive_delay</code>, then cancels the query. Counts appear in <code>pg_stat_database_conflicts</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','standby'] },
{ q: `What is the planner's statistics system (pg_statistic / pg_stats)?`,
  a: `<p><code>ANALYZE</code> samples 300 × <code>default_statistics_target</code> rows (default target 100 → 30,000 rows) per table and stores per-column statistics in <code>pg_statistic</code>, readable via <code>pg_stats</code>: <code>null_frac</code>, <code>avg_width</code>, <code>n_distinct</code>, <code>most_common_vals</code>/<code>most_common_freqs</code>, <code>histogram_bounds</code>, <code>correlation</code> (physical vs logical order). The planner multiplies selectivities assuming independence between columns, which extended statistics can correct. Raise targets for skewed columns: <code>ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN status SET STATISTICS 1000;</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','planner'] },
{ q: `What are extended statistics?`,
  a: `<p><code>CREATE STATISTICS</code> objects tell ANALYZE to gather multi-column statistics: <code>dependencies</code> (functional dependencies such as city → country), <code>ndistinct</code> (distinct counts for column combinations, improving GROUP BY estimates), and <code>mcv</code> (multi-column most-common values). Expression statistics are supported since PG 14.</p><pre>CREATE STATISTICS customers_geo (dependencies, mcv) ON country, city FROM customers;
ANALYZE customers;</pre><p>Without it, <code>WHERE country='India' AND city='Pune'</code> is estimated as product of the two selectivities, badly underestimating rows.</p>`,
  tags: ['statistics','planner'] },
{ q: `What is JIT compilation in PostgreSQL and when does it hurt?`,
  a: `<p>When built with LLVM, PostgreSQL can JIT-compile expression evaluation and tuple deforming for queries whose estimated cost exceeds <code>jit_above_cost</code> (100,000), with optimisation and inlining above higher thresholds. It helps long CPU-bound analytical queries; it hurts OLTP or mid-size queries that are estimated as expensive but actually fast, adding tens to hundreds of ms of compile time (visible in the "JIT:" section of EXPLAIN ANALYZE). Many OLTP deployments set <code>jit = off</code> or raise the thresholds.</p>`,
  tags: ['jit','performance','gucs'] },
{ q: `How does parallel query work?`,
  a: `<p>The leader launches background workers (up to <code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code>, default 2, from pools <code>max_parallel_workers</code> and <code>max_worker_processes</code>) for plan nodes below a <code>Gather</code> or <code>Gather Merge</code>. Supported: parallel seq/index/bitmap scans, parallel hash joins (sharing one hash table), partial aggregation, parallel append, parallel CREATE INDEX for B-tree (and BRIN in 17, GIN in 18). Not used for writes other than CREATE TABLE AS / CREATE INDEX / materialized views, inside functions marked <code>PARALLEL UNSAFE</code>, or below a cursor. Thresholds: <code>min_parallel_table_scan_size</code> (8 MB), <code>parallel_setup_cost</code>, <code>parallel_tuple_cost</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['parallel','performance'] },
{ q: `What is a prepared statement and generic vs custom plans?`,
  a: `<p><code>PREPARE</code> (or the extended protocol) parses and analyzes once. For the first five executions PostgreSQL builds <strong>custom plans</strong> using the actual parameter values; afterwards it compares their average cost with a <strong>generic plan</strong> (parameter-agnostic) and switches to the generic plan if it is not notably more expensive. With skewed data this can pick a plan bad for some values. <code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code> (or <code>force_generic_plan</code>) overrides per session/role. PgBouncer 1.21+ <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> supports protocol-level prepared statements in transaction mode via <code>max_prepared_statements</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','prepared-statements'] },
{ q: `What are useful timeouts to configure?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>statement_timeout</code>: cancel statements running longer than N.</li><li><code>lock_timeout</code>: give up waiting for a lock — essential for migrations.</li><li><code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code>: kill sessions idle inside an open transaction (they hold locks and the xmin horizon).</li><li><code>idle_session_timeout</code> (PG 14): kill idle connections — careful with poolers.</li><li><code>transaction_timeout</code> (PG 17): cap total transaction duration.</li><li><code>tcp_keepalives_*</code> and <code>client_connection_check_interval</code> (PG 14): detect dead clients.</li></ul><p>Set them per role (<code>ALTER ROLE web SET statement_timeout = '5s'</code>) rather than globally so admin jobs are not killed.</p>`,
  tags: ['gucs','operations'] },
{ q: `What is the xmin horizon and why do long transactions hurt everyone?`,
  a: `<p>The xmin horizon is the oldest transaction snapshot still needed anywhere: the minimum of active backends' <code>backend_xmin</code>, replication slots' <code>xmin</code>/<code>catalog_xmin</code>, prepared transactions, and standby feedback. VACUUM cannot remove any tuple deleted after that horizon. A single <code>BEGIN</code> left open for 6 hours (even idle) prevents cleanup of every dead row created in those 6 hours across the whole database — causing bloat, slower queries, index-only scans that hit the heap, and freezing delays.</p><pre>SELECT pid, state, backend_xmin, age(backend_xmin), now() - xact_start
FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE backend_xmin IS NOT NULL ORDER BY age(backend_xmin) DESC;</pre>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','vacuum','monitoring'] },
{ q: `What is the CLOG (pg_xact) and what are hint bits?`,
  a: `<p><code>pg_xact</code> stores 2 bits per transaction: in progress, committed, aborted, sub-committed. To decide visibility, PostgreSQL would need to look up a tuple's xmin/xmax status there; to avoid repeated lookups, the first reader that discovers a final status sets <strong>hint bits</strong> in the tuple header (<code>HEAP_XMIN_COMMITTED</code> etc.). Consequence: the first SELECT after a large bulk load can <strong>dirty and write</strong> many pages (and with checksums or <code>wal_log_hints</code>, emit full-page images). Running VACUUM after a bulk load sets hint bits and the visibility map in one pass.</p>`,
  tags: ['mvcc','internals'] },
{ q: `What is the background writer and how does it differ from the checkpointer?`,
  a: `<p>The <strong>checkpointer</strong> writes <em>all</em> dirty buffers periodically for recovery purposes. The <strong>background writer</strong> continuously writes some dirty buffers that are likely to be evicted soon (driven by <code>bgwriter_lru_maxpages</code>, <code>bgwriter_lru_multiplier</code>, <code>bgwriter_delay</code>), so that backends needing a free buffer rarely have to write a dirty one themselves. If <code>pg_stat_io</code> shows client backends doing many writes in context normal, the bgwriter is too timid or shared_buffers too small.</p>`,
  tags: ['process-model','io','checkpoint'] },
{ q: `What is pg_cron?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_cron (by Citus Data / Microsoft) is a cron-style job scheduler running inside PostgreSQL as a background worker: <code>SELECT cron.schedule('nightly-refresh', '0 2 * * *', 'REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY sales_mv');</code>. Requires <code>shared_preload_libraries</code>; jobs run in the database configured by <code>cron.database_name</code> (or use <code>cron.schedule_in_database</code>). Run history goes in <code>cron.job_run_details</code>. It is not core; on HA clusters the jobs run only on the primary.</p>`,
  tags: ['extensions','operations'] },
];

PGM.INTERVIEW.architecture = [
{ q: `Describe a typical PostgreSQL streaming replication architecture.`,
  a: `<p>One primary accepts writes. Each standby runs a <strong>WAL receiver</strong> that connects to a <strong>WAL sender</strong> on the primary (<code>primary_conninfo</code>, usually with a physical replication slot via <code>primary_slot_name</code>) and streams WAL continuously; the standby's startup process replays it. Standbys with <code>hot_standby = on</code> serve read-only queries. A standby is created from <code>pg_basebackup -R</code> (which writes <code>standby.signal</code> and connection settings). WAL archiving to object storage is added as a fallback so a standby that falls far behind can catch up from the archive. Monitor with <code>pg_stat_replication</code> on the primary and <code>pg_stat_wal_receiver</code> on standbys.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','ha'] },
{ q: `Synchronous vs asynchronous replication — trade-offs?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Asynchronous</strong> (default): commits return after the local WAL flush. Lowest latency, but a failover can lose the last few hundred ms–seconds of committed transactions (RPO &gt; 0). <strong>Synchronous</strong> (<code>synchronous_standby_names</code>): commit waits for a standby to confirm, giving RPO = 0 for those transactions, at the cost of a network round trip per commit (≈1 ms same AZ, 50–150 ms cross-region) and <strong>availability risk</strong> — if the only sync standby is down, writes hang. Production pattern: 1 primary + 2 standbys with quorum sync (<code>ANY 1 (s1, s2)</code>) in nearby zones, plus async replicas in a remote region for DR.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','ha','durability'] },
{ q: `What is quorum-based synchronous replication?`,
  a: `<pre>synchronous_standby_names = 'ANY 1 (pg_b, pg_c)'      # quorum
synchronous_standby_names = 'FIRST 1 (pg_b, pg_c)'    # priority</pre><p>With <code>ANY 1</code>, a commit waits until <em>any one</em> of the listed standbys confirms, so losing one standby does not stall writes. <code>FIRST n</code> uses priority order. Combined with <code>synchronous_commit = remote_apply</code>, readers on the confirming standby see the commit; with <code>on</code> it is durably flushed there. Patroni's <code>synchronous_mode</code> manages this list dynamically so it never names a dead standby.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','durability'] },
{ q: `How do you handle read-your-writes consistency with read replicas?`,
  a: `<p>Async replicas lag, so a user who updates their profile and immediately reloads may see stale data. Options:</p><ul><li>Route the user's reads to the primary for a short window after a write (session stickiness).</li><li>LSN-based routing: after a write, capture <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code>; before reading on a replica, check <code>pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() &gt;= that LSN</code> or fall back to the primary.</li><li>Use <code>synchronous_commit = remote_apply</code> for those specific transactions (costly).</li><li>Send only lag-tolerant queries (reports, search, listings) to replicas.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['replication','consistency','read-replicas'] },
{ q: `Define RPO and RTO and give realistic numbers for PostgreSQL setups.`,
  a: `<p><strong>RPO</strong> (Recovery Point Objective): how much data loss is acceptable. <strong>RTO</strong> (Recovery Time Objective): how long until service is restored.</p><ul><li>Nightly pg_dump only: RPO up to 24 h, RTO hours.</li><li>Base backups + continuous WAL archiving (pgBackRest): RPO ≈ seconds to one WAL segment / <code>archive_timeout</code>, RTO = restore + replay time (often 30 min–hours for TB).</li><li>Async streaming standby with Patroni: RPO seconds, RTO ≈ 30–60 s.</li><li>Quorum sync standbys: RPO 0, RTO ≈ 30 s.</li></ul><p>Design always starts from business-stated RPO/RTO, not from tooling.</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','dr','backup'] },
{ q: `How does Patroni provide high availability?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> Patroni is a Python daemon running beside each PostgreSQL node. It stores cluster state and a <strong>leader lock with a TTL</strong> in a distributed consensus store (DCS: etcd, Consul, ZooKeeper or Kubernetes API). The primary's Patroni renews the lock every loop; if it cannot (node or network failure), the lock expires, and the healthiest replica (least lag, within <code>maximum_lag_on_failover</code>) acquires it and promotes. A primary that loses the DCS <strong>demotes itself</strong>, preventing split brain. Patroni also manages config, <code>pg_rewind</code>, switchover, sync mode, and exposes a REST API that HAProxy health checks use for routing (<code>/primary</code>, <code>/replica</code>).</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','patroni','failover'] },
{ q: `What is split brain and how do you prevent it?`,
  a: `<p>Split brain is two nodes both accepting writes as primary, causing divergent data that is very hard to reconcile. Causes: network partition with naive failover scripts that promote a replica without ensuring the old primary stopped. Prevention: a <strong>consensus store with quorum</strong> (odd number of etcd nodes across 3 zones) so only one side can hold leadership; self-fencing (primary demotes when it loses the lock); STONITH or a watchdog (Patroni supports Linux softdog) to reboot a hung node; and client routing tied to the leader key rather than static IPs.</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','split-brain'] },
{ q: `Failover vs switchover?`,
  a: `<p>A <strong>switchover</strong> is planned: the primary is cleanly shut down after standbys have received all WAL, a chosen standby is promoted, the old primary rejoins as a standby — zero data loss, seconds of downtime, used for maintenance and OS patching. A <strong>failover</strong> is unplanned promotion after the primary fails; with async replication, transactions not yet received are lost, and the old primary usually needs <code>pg_rewind</code> before rejoining. Run switchovers regularly so failover paths are exercised.</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','failover'] },
{ q: `Compare HA tooling options for PostgreSQL.`,
  a: `<ul><li><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> <strong>Patroni</strong>: de facto standard on VMs/bare metal; DCS-based, mature.</li><li><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> <strong>CloudNativePG</strong>: Kubernetes operator; uses the Kubernetes API for leader election, built-in backups to object storage.</li><li><strong>pg_auto_failover</strong> (Microsoft): monitor node plus keeper; simple but monitor is a component to protect.</li><li><strong>repmgr</strong>: older, script-based; weaker split-brain protection.</li><li>Managed services (RDS Multi-AZ, Aurora, Cloud SQL HA, Azure Flexible Server): HA outsourced, limited control.</li></ul><p>PostgreSQL core provides replication and promotion (<code>pg_promote()</code>), but no automatic failover — that always comes from tooling.</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','tools'] },
{ q: `How do applications find the current primary after failover?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>libpq multi-host</strong> (core): <code>host=pg1,pg2,pg3 target_session_attrs=read-write</code> tries hosts until it finds a primary; <code>prefer-standby</code>/<code>read-only</code> for reads; <code>load_balance_hosts=random</code> (PG 16) spreads connections. JDBC has equivalent options.</li><li><strong>HAProxy</strong> <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> health-checking Patroni's REST API on separate ports for primary and replicas.</li><li><strong>Virtual IP</strong> moved by keepalived or vip-manager (same L2 network).</li><li><strong>DNS</strong> update (managed services) — beware TTL caching in JVMs.</li></ul><p>Whatever you choose, apps must reconnect and retry on failure.</p>`,
  tags: ['ha','routing','connections'] },
{ q: `What is cascading replication and when would you use it?`,
  a: `<p>A standby that streams WAL to other standbys (it has its own WAL senders). It reduces load and egress on the primary and cross-region bandwidth: ship WAL once to a remote region's first standby, which fans out to local replicas. Downsides: more lag hops and a dependency chain — if the intermediate node fails, downstream replicas must be repointed (Patroni handles this via <code>replicatefrom</code> tags). Cascading standbys can also be synchronous only to their upstream's degree; sync commit applies only to direct standbys of the primary.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','dr'] },
{ q: `What is a delayed replica and why keep one?`,
  a: `<p>A standby with <code>recovery_min_apply_delay = '4h'</code> receives WAL immediately but applies it only after the delay. If someone runs <code>DROP TABLE</code> or a bad <code>UPDATE</code> without WHERE, you have hours to pause replay (<code>SELECT pg_wal_replay_pause();</code>), recover to just before the mistake, and extract the data — much faster than restoring a multi-TB backup. It does not replace backups and must never be a failover candidate.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','dr','backup'] },
{ q: `Design a multi-region DR setup for a PostgreSQL OLTP system.`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Region A</strong> (primary): 3 nodes across 3 AZs, Patroni with etcd, quorum sync <code>ANY 1</code> between the two local standbys — RPO 0 for zone failures, RTO &lt; 1 min.</li><li><strong>Region B</strong> (DR): a Patroni <em>standby cluster</em> (its leader is a standby that cascades to a local replica) fed asynchronously — RPO seconds.</li><li><strong>Backups</strong>: pgBackRest to object storage replicated cross-region; PITR window 14–30 days.</li><li><strong>Runbook</strong>: region promotion is a manual, human decision (avoid flapping across regions), DNS/connection string change, sequence and app config checks; tested quarterly.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['dr','ha','multi-region'] },
{ q: `Can PostgreSQL do multi-master (active-active) replication?`,
  a: `<p>Not in core. Core logical replication can be configured bidirectionally (PG 16 added <code>origin = none</code> to avoid loops), but there is <strong>no conflict resolution</strong> — conflicting writes break replication. Commercial/third-party options include EDB Postgres Distributed (BDR) and pgEdge, which add conflict handling (last-update-wins, CRDTs). Active-active only works well when writes are partitioned (each region owns its rows) or conflicts are rare and well understood; global uniqueness, sequences and constraints become hard. Most systems are better served by single-writer plus fast failover, or a distributed SQL database.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','multi-master'] },
{ q: `How does the CAP theorem apply to a PostgreSQL primary-replica cluster?`,
  a: `<p>During a network partition, a system must choose Consistency or Availability. A single-primary PostgreSQL cluster with Patroni is <strong>CP</strong>: the side without DCS quorum refuses writes (primary demotes). With sync replication and no reachable sync standby, writes block (choosing consistency over availability). Async replicas serving reads during a partition offer availability with stale reads — an AP behaviour for reads. PACELC adds: even without partitions, you trade Latency vs Consistency — e.g. async (low latency) vs <code>remote_apply</code> (consistent reads, higher latency).</p>`,
  tags: ['cap','distributed'] },
{ q: `When do you actually need sharding?`,
  a: `<p>Only after vertical scaling and simpler measures are exhausted: a modern server with 64–192 vCPUs, 1–2 TB RAM and NVMe can run multi-TB PostgreSQL with tens of thousands of TPS. Exhaust first: query/index tuning, read replicas for read load, partitioning for maintenance, archiving cold data, caching, and moving analytics elsewhere. Real triggers for sharding: <strong>write throughput or WAL volume</strong> beyond a single node, working set far larger than RAM with latency SLOs, data so large that restores/vacuum exceed operational windows, or tenant/data-residency isolation. Sharding adds permanent complexity: cross-shard queries, transactions, rebalancing.</p>`,
  tags: ['sharding','scaling'] },
{ q: `How do you choose a shard key?`,
  a: `<p>A good shard key: (1) appears in nearly every query so queries route to one shard; (2) has high cardinality and even distribution to avoid hot shards; (3) groups data that is joined and transacted together (co-location). In B2B SaaS this is usually <code>tenant_id</code>/<code>company_id</code>; in consumer apps, <code>user_id</code>. Bad keys: timestamps (all writes hit the newest shard), low-cardinality columns (country), or keys that a few giant customers dominate (plan tenant isolation for whales). Changing the shard key later is a full data migration, so model it up front.</p>`,
  tags: ['sharding','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `Explain the Citus architecture.`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> Citus turns PostgreSQL into a distributed database (it is not native partitioning). A <strong>coordinator</strong> node holds metadata and plans queries; <strong>worker</strong> nodes hold <strong>shards</strong> — ordinary PostgreSQL tables like <code>orders_102008</code>. Table types: <strong>distributed tables</strong> (<code>create_distributed_table('orders','customer_id')</code>, hash-sharded, 32 shards by default), <strong>reference tables</strong> (small tables replicated to every worker for local joins), and local tables. Tables distributed on the same column with the same shard count are <strong>co-located</strong>, so joins and FK constraints on the distribution key run locally on each worker. Queries filtered by distribution key are routed to one shard; others are parallelized across all shards and merged.</p>`,
  tags: ['citus','sharding','extensions'] },
{ q: `What is schema-based sharding in Citus?`,
  a: `<p>Introduced in Citus 12: with <code>citus.enable_schema_based_sharding = on</code>, each new schema becomes a <strong>distributed schema</strong> whose tables live together on one node, and different schemas are spread across nodes. It suits schema-per-tenant SaaS and microservices: applications need no distribution column changes, just <code>search_path</code>. Trade-off versus row-based sharding: a single large tenant cannot be spread across nodes, and cross-tenant queries are harder.</p>`,
  tags: ['citus','sharding','multi-tenant'] },
{ q: `What are the limitations and pitfalls of Citus?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Queries without the distribution key fan out to all shards — fine for analytics, expensive for high-QPS OLTP.</li><li>Joins between non-co-located distributed tables require repartitioning (slow or disallowed depending on settings).</li><li>Unique constraints and primary keys must include the distribution column.</li><li>Some SQL features have restrictions (certain correlated subqueries, some DDL patterns).</li><li>Operational complexity: rebalancing (<code>citus_rebalance_start()</code>), version upgrades across nodes, HA per node (each worker needs its own replicas).</li><li>Connection fan-out: one coordinator query may open many worker connections — tune <code>citus.max_adaptive_executor_pool_size</code>.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['citus','sharding'] },
{ q: `Application-level sharding vs Citus vs distributed SQL — how do you choose?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Application-level sharding</strong> (app picks a database by tenant, a lookup directory maps tenants to clusters): full control, plain PostgreSQL everywhere, but the app owns routing, cross-shard reporting and rebalancing. <strong>Citus</strong> <span class="tag ext">extension</span>: transparent SQL routing and parallel queries, keeps the PostgreSQL ecosystem, good for multi-tenant SaaS and real-time analytics. <strong>Distributed SQL</strong> (CockroachDB, YugabyteDB, Spanner — PostgreSQL-compatible wire protocol but different engines): automatic sharding and multi-region consistency via consensus, at the cost of higher per-query latency and incomplete PostgreSQL compatibility (extensions, some features). Pick based on query patterns, team expertise and how global the writes must be.</p>`,
  tags: ['sharding','citus','distributed-sql'] },
{ q: `How do you rebalance or reshard data with minimal downtime?`,
  a: `<p>Avoid mapping keys directly to N physical nodes (<code>hash % N</code> forces moving almost everything when N changes). Instead use many <strong>logical shards</strong> (e.g. 256 or 1024) mapped onto fewer nodes via a directory; adding a node moves whole logical shards. The move itself: copy the shard with logical replication (initial copy + change stream), wait for catch-up, block writes to that shard briefly, flip the routing entry, resume. Citus does exactly this with <code>citus_move_shard_placement</code> / the rebalancer, using logical replication so writes continue. Consistent hashing is an alternative when there is no directory.</p>`,
  tags: ['sharding','rebalancing'] },
{ q: `How do you generate globally unique IDs in a sharded system?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>UUIDv7</strong> (core <code>uuidv7()</code> in PG 18, or generated in the app): unique without coordination, time-ordered for index locality, 16 bytes.</li><li><strong>Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs</strong>: timestamp + shard/node ID + sequence; compact bigint, sortable; needs clock discipline.</li><li><strong>Per-shard sequences with offsets</strong> (<code>INCREMENT BY 1024 START WITH shard_no</code>): simple but rigid when shard count changes.</li><li><strong>Embed the shard key</strong> in the ID so the shard can be derived from any ID.</li></ul><p>Avoid random UUIDv4 as clustered keys on huge tables — poor B-tree locality.</p>`,
  tags: ['sharding','ids'] },
{ q: `What are the declarative partitioning strategies?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>RANGE</strong>: time series — <code>PARTITION BY RANGE (order_date)</code> with monthly partitions; enables dropping old data instantly.</li><li><strong>LIST</strong>: discrete values — region, tenant tier, status.</li><li><strong>HASH</strong>: <code>PARTITION BY HASH (customer_id)</code> with <code>MODULUS 16, REMAINDER n</code>; spreads data evenly to keep partitions small for vacuum/index maintenance when there is no natural range.</li></ul><p>Partitions can be sub-partitioned, can be foreign tables (postgres_fdw), and indexes created on the parent cascade to all partitions. Choose a key that appears in most WHERE clauses so pruning works.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning'] },
{ q: `How does partition pruning work?`,
  a: `<p>The planner excludes partitions whose bounds cannot match the WHERE clause (<code>enable_partition_pruning = on</code>). <strong>Plan-time pruning</strong> works with constants. <strong>Execution-time pruning</strong> (PG 11+) handles parameters from prepared statements, subqueries and nested-loop joins, shown in EXPLAIN as <code>Subplans Removed: N</code>. Pruning fails when the predicate wraps the key in a function or a different type: <code>WHERE date_trunc('month', order_date) = ...</code> or comparing a <code>timestamptz</code> key with values requiring non-immutable casts. Write <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2025-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-02-01'</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','planner'] },
{ q: `Partitioning vs sharding — what is the difference?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Partitioning</strong> splits one logical table into child tables <em>on the same server</em>: it helps maintenance (drop old partitions instead of DELETE, smaller vacuums and indexes) and pruning, but does not add CPU, RAM or write capacity. <strong>Sharding</strong> spreads data across <em>multiple servers</em>, adding capacity but introducing distributed-systems problems. Citus combines both ideas (distributed tables whose shards can themselves be partitioned). Many teams reach for sharding when partitioning plus archiving would have sufficed.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','sharding'] },
{ q: `How many partitions is too many?`,
  a: `<p>Modern PostgreSQL (13+) handles thousands of partitions when queries prune to a few, but costs grow with count: planning time and memory for queries that cannot prune, relcache memory per backend that touches many partitions, lock manager pressure (each partition and index needs a lock; fast-path locks are limited — PG 18 raised the limit, scaled with <code>max_locks_per_transaction</code>), and more files. Rule of thumb: keep partitions in the hundreds to low thousands, sized so each is from a few GB to ~100 GB. Daily partitions for 10 years (3,650) are usually worse than monthly.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','performance'] },
{ q: `What are the pitfalls of a DEFAULT partition?`,
  a: `<p>A default partition catches rows that fit no other partition, preventing insert errors. But when you later create or attach a partition, PostgreSQL must <strong>scan the default partition</strong> to verify none of its rows belong to the new range — under a lock — which is slow if the default has grown. Also, rows silently landing there hide bugs (e.g. missing future partitions). Prefer pre-creating partitions ahead of time (pg_partman <span class="tag ext">extension</span> or a scheduled job) and alert if the default is non-empty.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning'] },
{ q: `What is pg_partman?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_partman automates time- and integer-based partition management on top of native declarative partitioning: creating future partitions in advance (<code>premake</code>), dropping or detaching partitions beyond a retention period, and moving data out of the default partition. Run maintenance with <code>partman.run_maintenance_proc()</code> from its background worker or pg_cron. It is not core and versions 5.x dropped trigger-based partitioning support.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','extensions'] },
{ q: `Why must unique constraints on a partitioned table include the partition key?`,
  a: `<p>PostgreSQL has no global indexes: each partition has its own index, so uniqueness can only be enforced within a partition. If the partition key is part of the unique key, two equal keys must land in the same partition, so local enforcement is sufficient. Thus a table partitioned by <code>order_date</code> cannot have <code>PRIMARY KEY (id)</code> — it needs <code>(id, order_date)</code>. If you need global uniqueness of <code>id</code>, use a globally unique generator (identity/UUIDv7) and accept the composite PK, or maintain a separate lookup table.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','constraints'] },
{ q: `How do you implement data retention on a partitioned table without impacting production?`,
  a: `<pre>ALTER TABLE events DETACH PARTITION events_2023_01 CONCURRENTLY;  -- PG 14+
-- archive: COPY / pg_dump the detached table to object storage
DROP TABLE events_2023_01;</pre><p><code>DETACH ... CONCURRENTLY</code> uses a weaker lock and two transactions so queries keep running (it cannot run inside a transaction block and not with a default partition). Compared with <code>DELETE FROM events WHERE ts &lt; ...</code> — which generates huge WAL, dead tuples and vacuum work — dropping a partition is a near-instant metadata operation.</p>`,
  tags: ['partitioning','retention'] },
{ q: `Compare multi-tenancy models in PostgreSQL.`,
  a: `<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr><tr><td>Database per tenant</td><td>Strong isolation, easy per-tenant backup/restore and residency</td><td>Connections per database, painful migrations across thousands of DBs, poor resource sharing</td></tr><tr><td>Schema per tenant</td><td>Logical isolation, easy tenant drop</td><td>Catalog bloat beyond a few thousand schemas, migration fan-out, pg_dump slowness</td></tr><tr><td>Shared tables with <code>tenant_id</code></td><td>Scales to millions of tenants, one migration, efficient</td><td>Isolation depends on app or RLS, noisy neighbours, needs <code>tenant_id</code> in every index</td></tr></table><p>Common answer: shared tables + RLS for the long tail, with the option to move whales to dedicated clusters or Citus nodes.</p>`,
  tags: ['multi-tenant','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `Why does schema-per-tenant break down at scale?`,
  a: `<p>Every table, index and sequence per schema adds rows to <code>pg_class</code>, <code>pg_attribute</code>, etc. With 10,000 tenants × 50 tables × 3 indexes you have millions of relations: catalog caches per backend grow large (memory per connection), <code>pg_dump</code> and <code>pg_upgrade</code> slow dramatically, autovacuum must cycle through millions of small tables, and migrations must run 10,000 times with partial-failure handling. It works well for hundreds to low thousands of tenants.</p>`,
  tags: ['multi-tenant','scaling'] },
{ q: `How do you handle noisy neighbours in a multi-tenant PostgreSQL system?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Per-role limits: <code>statement_timeout</code>, <code>work_mem</code>, connection limit (<code>ALTER ROLE ... CONNECTION LIMIT</code>) and per-tenant pool sizes in the pooler.</li><li>Rate limiting at the API layer per tenant.</li><li>Separate heavy workloads: exports and analytics on replicas.</li><li>Monitor per-tenant cost (application_name or query comments, pg_stat_statements per role).</li><li>Isolate whales: move them to dedicated shards/clusters (Citus <code>isolate_tenant_to_new_shard</code>).</li></ul><p>PostgreSQL has no built-in CPU/IO resource governor per tenant; OS cgroups apply per cluster, not per query.</p>`,
  tags: ['multi-tenant','operations'] },
{ q: `How do you combine RLS-based tenant isolation with connection pooling?`,
  a: `<p>Pooled connections are shared, so tenant context must be set <strong>per transaction</strong>, not per session:</p><pre>BEGIN;
SELECT set_config('app.tenant_id', '42', true);  -- true = transaction-local
SELECT * FROM invoices;                          -- RLS policy uses current_setting('app.tenant_id')
COMMIT;</pre><p>The app connects as a non-owner role without <code>BYPASSRLS</code>; tables use <code>ENABLE</code> (and ideally <code>FORCE</code>) ROW LEVEL SECURITY; policies compare <code>tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::bigint</code>; every index leads with <code>tenant_id</code>. Using session-level <code>SET</code> with PgBouncer transaction pooling would leak tenant context to other clients.</p>`,
  tags: ['multi-tenant','rls','pooling'] },
{ q: `Explain PgBouncer pooling modes.`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> PgBouncer is a lightweight connection pooler.</p><ul><li><strong>Session</strong>: a server connection is assigned for the whole client session — safe for all features, but does little beyond limiting connection churn.</li><li><strong>Transaction</strong>: a server connection is assigned per transaction and returned at COMMIT — thousands of clients share tens of server connections. Breaks session state: session <code>SET</code>, session advisory locks, <code>LISTEN</code>, <code>WITH HOLD</code> cursors, temp tables across transactions (protocol-level prepared statements are supported from 1.21 with <code>max_prepared_statements</code>).</li><li><strong>Statement</strong>: per statement; multi-statement transactions disallowed.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['pooling','pgbouncer'] },
{ q: `How do you size a connection pool?`,
  a: `<p>Start from the database: active connections beyond roughly 2–4 × CPU cores (more if I/O-bound) add contention, not throughput. For a 16-core server, a total server-side pool of ~40–80 is typical. Then divide across app instances, remembering autoscaling: 50 pods × pool 20 = 1,000 connections, which is why a central PgBouncer between them is common. Set <code>default_pool_size</code>, <code>max_client_conn</code> (high, e.g. 5000), <code>reserve_pool_size</code>, and keep <code>max_connections</code> slightly above total pool sizes plus admin/replication headroom. Measure: if clients wait in the pooler (<code>cl_waiting</code> in <code>SHOW POOLS</code>) while DB CPU is low, pool too small; if DB CPU saturates, more connections will not help.</p>`,
  tags: ['pooling','capacity'] },
{ q: `Where should a connection pooler run?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>In the app</strong> (HikariCP, SQLAlchemy pool): low latency, but every app instance holds its own pool — total connections multiply with replicas.</li><li><strong>Sidecar</strong> PgBouncer per app host/pod: bounds per-host connections; still multiplies with hosts.</li><li><strong>Central</strong> PgBouncer layer near the database (on the DB host or dedicated hosts behind a load balancer): global cap on server connections, single place to manage; must itself be HA and can be a bottleneck (PgBouncer is single-threaded, so run several instances with <code>so_reuseport</code>).</li></ul><p>Large systems often combine an app pool (small) with a central pooler.</p>`,
  tags: ['pooling','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do serverless functions (e.g. AWS Lambda) cause connection problems and what are the fixes?`,
  a: `<p>Each concurrent function instance opens its own connection; a burst to 2,000 concurrent invocations means 2,000 PostgreSQL backends, exhausting <code>max_connections</code> and memory, and connections are left idle in frozen containers. Fixes: a managed proxy (RDS Proxy, Azure's built-in PgBouncer), a self-hosted PgBouncer in transaction mode, HTTP-based data APIs (Neon serverless driver, Supabase/Supavisor), capping function concurrency, and opening connections outside the handler for reuse across warm invocations.</p>`,
  tags: ['pooling','serverless'] },
{ q: `PgBouncer vs Pgpool-II vs newer poolers?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>s, none in core. <strong>PgBouncer</strong>: tiny, fast, reliable pooling only; single-threaded. <strong>Pgpool-II</strong>: pooling plus load balancing reads to replicas, query routing, its own failover/watchdog — powerful but complex and SQL-parsing based. <strong>Multi-threaded newcomers</strong>: Odyssey (Yandex), PgCat (sharding and replica routing, Rust), Supavisor (Elixir, multi-tenant, used by Supabase). Typical recommendation: PgBouncer for pooling, HAProxy or libpq multi-host for routing, Patroni for HA — each tool doing one job.</p>`,
  tags: ['pooling','tools'] },
{ q: `Design a backup strategy for a 5 TB production PostgreSQL database.`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Physical backups</strong> with <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> pgBackRest (or Barman): weekly full, daily differential/incremental, parallel and compressed (zstd/lz4), encrypted, to object storage in another region; taken from a standby to offload the primary.</li><li><strong>Continuous WAL archiving</strong> (<code>archive-push</code> asynchronous) for PITR with RPO of seconds.</li><li><strong>Retention</strong>: e.g. 4 full backups / 30 days PITR, plus monthly copies kept longer for compliance.</li><li><strong>Logical dumps</strong> of critical small schemas for object-level restores and portability.</li><li><strong>Verification</strong>: automated weekly restore to a scratch server, run checks (amcheck, row counts), record actual RTO.</li><li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: archive failures (<code>pg_stat_archiver.failed_count</code>), backup age alerts.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['backup','dr','pgbackrest'] },
{ q: `Why is a replica not a backup?`,
  a: `<p>Replication faithfully copies mistakes: a <code>DROP TABLE</code>, a buggy migration, a ransomware <code>UPDATE</code>, or logical corruption are replayed to every standby within milliseconds. Replicas protect against hardware/zone failure (availability), while backups with PITR protect against data loss from human/software error (recoverability). A delayed replica helps but still is not an off-site, versioned, immutable copy. You need both.</p>`,
  tags: ['backup','replication'] },
{ q: `Can you use storage/volume snapshots as PostgreSQL backups?`,
  a: `<p>Yes, if done correctly. An atomic snapshot of <strong>all</strong> volumes containing the data directory and <code>pg_wal</code> at the same instant is crash-consistent — restoring it is like recovering from a power loss. If data and WAL are on different volumes, or for PITR use, wrap the snapshot with <code>SELECT pg_backup_start('snap', fast =&gt; true);</code> and <code>SELECT * FROM pg_backup_stop();</code> (PG 15+ names; non-exclusive mode, the session must stay open) and store the returned <code>backup_label</code> content with the snapshot, plus archived WAL. Snapshots are fast for huge databases but usually stay in the same cloud account/region — copy them elsewhere.</p>`,
  tags: ['backup','snapshots'] },
{ q: `How do you verify backups are restorable?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Automated scheduled restores to an isolated host (pgBackRest <code>restore</code>, then start PostgreSQL and replay to a target time).</li><li>Integrity checks: <code>pgbackrest verify</code>, <code>pg_verifybackup</code> (core, checks the backup manifest), and the core <span class="tag ext">extension</span> amcheck (<code>bt_index_check</code>, <code>verify_heapam</code>) or <code>pg_amcheck</code> on the restored copy.</li><li>Application-level checks: row counts, latest timestamps, a smoke test of critical queries.</li><li>Record actual restore duration and compare with the RTO target; alert if it grows.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['backup','dr'] },
{ q: `Normalize or denormalize at scale?`,
  a: `<p>Start normalized (3NF): one source of truth, smaller rows, integrity via FKs. Denormalize deliberately where measurements justify it: hot read paths that join 6 tables at 10k QPS, counters (<code>comment_count</code>) that would otherwise be computed with <code>COUNT(*)</code>, or analytics. Keep denormalized copies consistent with triggers, the same transaction, or asynchronous projections (CDC) where staleness is acceptable — and document which is the source of truth. Materialized views and covering indexes (<code>INCLUDE</code>) often give the speed without schema duplication.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','normalization'] },
{ q: `When should you use JSONB columns instead of relational columns, and when is EAV appropriate?`,
  a: `<p>Use <strong>JSONB</strong> for genuinely variable or sparse attributes (product specs by category, webhook payloads, user preferences), data read as a whole document, or schemas owned by external systems. Keep in proper columns anything you filter, join, aggregate, constrain or update frequently — the planner has statistics on columns, not JSON keys, and JSONB updates rewrite the whole value. <strong>EAV</strong> (entity-attribute-value tables) is rarely appropriate in PostgreSQL now: it destroys typing and constraints and needs pivots for every query; JSONB with GIN indexes replaces most EAV use cases.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','jsonb'] },
{ q: `How would you design a time-series store in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Range-partition by time (daily/weekly/monthly, sized to keep partitions manageable), retention by dropping partitions.</li><li><strong>BRIN</strong> indexes on the timestamp (tiny, effective because data arrives in time order) plus B-tree on (series_id, ts) for point lookups.</li><li>Batch inserts (<code>COPY</code> or multi-row INSERT), minimal secondary indexes.</li><li>Pre-aggregated rollup tables (per minute/hour) maintained incrementally.</li><li>Consider <span class="tag ext">extension</span> TimescaleDB: hypertables with automatic chunking, native compression (columnar, 90%+ savings), continuous aggregates, retention policies.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['time-series','partitioning','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `How do you design an append-only ledger (e.g. wallet balances) in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Immutable <code>ledger_entries</code> table (double-entry: each transfer writes a debit and a credit summing to zero), never updated or deleted — revoke UPDATE/DELETE from the app role.</li><li>Idempotency key with a unique constraint to make retries safe.</li><li>Current balance either computed (<code>SUM</code> over entries, with periodic snapshots) or maintained in an <code>accounts</code> row updated in the same transaction with <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> to serialize concurrent spends, plus a <code>CHECK (balance &gt;= 0)</code>.</li><li>Lock accounts in a consistent order (lower id first) to avoid deadlocks on transfers.</li><li>Partition entries by time for retention/archival; keep reconciliation jobs comparing sums with snapshots.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','fintech','patterns'] },
{ q: `What are the trade-offs of soft deletes at scale?`,
  a: `<p>A <code>deleted_at</code> column keeps rows for undo and audit, but every query must filter it (bugs leak deleted data), unique constraints must become partial (<code>CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users(email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL</code>), tables and indexes keep growing with dead business data, and FKs still reference "deleted" rows. Alternatives: move deleted rows to an archive table in the same transaction (or via trigger), use partial indexes for live rows, and apply RLS or views to hide deleted rows. For privacy (GDPR erasure) soft delete is not sufficient.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','patterns'] },
{ q: `How do you implement audit logging, and what are the architecture choices?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Row triggers</strong> writing old/new rows as JSONB into an <code>audit_logs</code> table: transactional and simple; adds write latency and doubles write volume; can capture app user via <code>current_setting('app.user_id', true)</code>.</li><li><strong>Logical decoding / CDC</strong> (Debezium <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>) to a separate store: no overhead in the write transaction, scales better, but asynchronous and needs the user context written into the row.</li><li><strong>pgAudit</strong> <span class="tag ext">extension</span>: statement/object-level audit logging to the server log for compliance (who ran what), not row history.</li></ul><p>Partition audit tables by time and ship old partitions to cheap storage.</p>`,
  tags: ['audit','cdc','patterns'] },
{ q: `What is the transactional outbox pattern?`,
  a: `<p>Problem: a service must update its database <em>and</em> publish an event (Kafka, SQS); doing both separately risks one succeeding without the other (dual write). Solution: in the same database transaction as the business change, insert the event into an <code>outbox</code> table. A relay then publishes outbox rows — either polling with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> and marking them sent, or via CDC (Debezium's outbox event router reading WAL). Delivery is at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. Keep the outbox small: delete or partition-drop processed rows.</p>`,
  tags: ['patterns','events','microservices'] },
{ q: `How does CQRS apply with PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>Command Query Responsibility Segregation separates the write model (normalized OLTP tables with constraints) from read models optimized for queries (denormalized tables, materialized views, search indexes, or a different store). Read models are updated synchronously (triggers, same transaction) or asynchronously (outbox/CDC projections) with eventual consistency. In PostgreSQL you can often get most of the benefit cheaply: read replicas for query load, materialized views refreshed concurrently, and summary tables — moving to separate stores only when query shapes truly diverge (full-text search, analytics).</p>`,
  tags: ['patterns','cqrs'] },
{ q: `How do you add a caching layer (e.g. Redis) in front of PostgreSQL safely?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Cache-aside</strong>: read from cache, on miss read DB and populate with a TTL; on write, update DB then <strong>delete</strong> the cache key (not update, to avoid races writing stale values).</li><li>Protect against stampedes on popular keys (request coalescing, early refresh, jittered TTLs).</li><li>Invalidation sources: app code after commit, or CDC/LISTEN-NOTIFY so all writers invalidate consistently.</li><li>Cache only data that tolerates staleness; never cache authorisation decisions longer than acceptable.</li></ul><p>First check whether PostgreSQL itself is the bottleneck — a well-indexed lookup served from shared buffers is sub-millisecond, and caching adds consistency bugs.</p>`,
  tags: ['caching','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do you design around hot rows such as a global counter or a popular item's like count?`,
  a: `<p>Every update to one row serializes on its row lock and creates a new tuple version, so a single counter row caps throughput at a few thousand updates per second and generates bloat. Options:</p><ul><li><strong>Sharded counters</strong>: N rows per counter (<code>slot = random() * N</code>), sum on read.</li><li><strong>Append + aggregate</strong>: insert events and roll them up periodically into the counter.</li><li><strong>Buffer in Redis</strong> and flush increments in batches.</li><li>Approximate where acceptable.</li></ul><p>Also keep counter rows in narrow tables with a low fillfactor to keep updates HOT.</p>`,
  tags: ['contention','patterns'] },
{ q: `bigint identity vs UUID primary keys — which do you choose?`,
  a: `<p><strong>bigint identity</strong>: 8 bytes, compact indexes, sequential inserts (right-most B-tree page stays in cache), easy debugging; but reveals counts/ordering, needs the database to issue IDs, and is awkward to merge across shards. <strong>UUIDv4</strong>: globally unique and client-generatable but 16 bytes and random — index inserts scatter across the tree, causing more page splits, cache misses and WAL. <strong>UUIDv7</strong> (core <code>uuidv7()</code> from PG 18) gives global uniqueness with near-sequential locality. Choose bigint for single-database OLTP, UUIDv7 when IDs are generated in distributed services or exposed publicly; avoid v4 as the clustered key of very large tables.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','ids'] },
{ q: `Should you keep foreign keys at large scale?`,
  a: `<p>Generally yes: they prevent orphaned data that is very expensive to clean later. Costs to manage: each child insert/update does a lookup on the parent's unique index and takes a <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code> lock; parent deletes/updates scan the child — so <strong>always index FK columns</strong> on the child; adding an FK to a big table should use <code>NOT VALID</code> then <code>VALIDATE CONSTRAINT</code>. Teams drop FKs mainly across shard boundaries (not enforceable) or on extreme-ingest event tables. Citus supports FKs between co-located tables and to reference tables.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','constraints'] },
{ q: `How do you archive cold data from a large OLTP database?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Partition by time so old data lives in separate partitions; detach and export them (COPY to Parquet/CSV in object storage, or move to an archive database).</li><li>For unpartitioned tables, move in batches: <code>WITH moved AS (DELETE FROM orders WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM orders WHERE order_date &lt; ... LIMIT 10000) RETURNING *) INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM moved;</code> committing per batch, throttled, with vacuum keeping up.</li><li>Keep archived data queryable when needed: a separate PostgreSQL instance, postgres_fdw, or a lakehouse engine reading Parquet.</li><li>Agree retention rules with legal/compliance first.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['archiving','data-lifecycle'] },
{ q: `When should analytics move off the OLTP PostgreSQL database?`,
  a: `<p>When analytical queries scan large fractions of tables, run for minutes, compete for CPU/IO/buffer cache with transactional traffic, or need columnar speed over billions of rows. Steps along the path: run reports on a replica (beware <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> bloat vs query cancellations); then CDC (logical replication/Debezium) or batch ELT into a columnar warehouse (ClickHouse, BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift) or a lakehouse. In-PostgreSQL options exist as <span class="tag ext">extension</span>s: Citus columnar, TimescaleDB compression, and DuckDB-based extensions (pg_duckdb, pg_mooncake) for columnar analytics.</p>`,
  tags: ['olap','cdc','architecture'] },
{ q: `PostgreSQL full-text search vs Elasticsearch/OpenSearch?`,
  a: `<p>Core <strong>full-text search</strong> (<code>tsvector</code>, <code>tsquery</code>, GIN index, <code>ts_rank</code>) is transactional, consistent with your data, needs no extra infrastructure, and handles millions of documents well. Add pg_trgm for fuzzy/typo matching. Choose a dedicated engine when you need BM25-quality relevance tuning at large scale, faceting/aggregations over huge corpora, many languages with advanced analyzers, or search QPS that should not load the primary database. The cost is a separate cluster plus a sync pipeline (CDC) with eventual consistency. Extensions like ParadeDB's pg_search bring BM25 into PostgreSQL as a middle ground.</p>`,
  tags: ['search','fts','architecture'] },
{ q: `Describe a RAG architecture using PostgreSQL and pgvector.`,
  a: `<ol><li><strong>Ingest</strong>: extract text from documents, split into chunks (e.g. 300–800 tokens with overlap), store chunk text + metadata (document_id, tenant_id, section, ACLs, timestamps).</li><li><strong>Embed</strong>: call an embedding model; store the vector in a <code>vector(n)</code> column (<span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgvector) in the same row as the chunk.</li><li><strong>Index</strong>: HNSW with the matching operator class (<code>vector_cosine_ops</code>), plus B-tree on filter columns and a tsvector/GIN for keyword search.</li><li><strong>Retrieve</strong>: embed the user question, run <code>ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT k</code> with SQL filters (tenant, permissions), optionally hybrid with full-text and re-rank.</li><li><strong>Generate</strong>: pass the top chunks with citations to the LLM.</li></ol><p>Advantage of PostgreSQL: one transactional store with joins, RLS and backups — no sync to a separate vector database.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','rag','ai'] },
{ q: `HNSW vs IVFFlat indexes in pgvector?`,
  a: `<p>Both are approximate nearest-neighbour indexes from the <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgvector.</p><ul><li><strong>HNSW</strong>: multi-layer proximity graph. Better recall/speed trade-off, works on an empty table (no training), handles inserts incrementally. Slower and memory-hungry builds (set <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> high; parallel builds supported). Tuning: <code>m</code>, <code>ef_construction</code> at build; <code>hnsw.ef_search</code> (default 40) at query time.</li><li><strong>IVFFlat</strong>: k-means clusters (<code>lists</code>), searching <code>ivfflat.probes</code> nearest clusters. Faster to build and smaller, but must be built after loading representative data and recall drifts as data changes.</li></ul><p>Default choice today: HNSW.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','indexes','ai'] },
{ q: `What is the problem with filtered vector search, and how does pgvector address it?`,
  a: `<p>An ANN index returns the approximately nearest <code>ef_search</code> candidates, and the WHERE filter (e.g. <code>tenant_id = 42</code>) is applied afterwards. If only 1% of rows match, most candidates are discarded and you may get fewer than <code>LIMIT</code> results ("overfiltering"). Remedies: pgvector 0.8+ <strong>iterative index scans</strong> (<code>SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order</code> / <code>strict_order</code>) that keep scanning until enough rows pass; partial indexes per large tenant or category; partitioning by tenant so each partition has its own index; or for highly selective filters, let the planner use a B-tree on the filter and compute exact distances on the small set.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','ai','performance'] },
{ q: `How do you estimate storage for embeddings?`,
  a: `<p>A <code>vector(n)</code> uses 4 bytes per dimension + 8 bytes header. 1,536 dimensions ≈ 6 KB per row; 10M chunks ≈ 61 GB for the vectors alone, plus an HNSW index of similar or larger size. Note 6 KB exceeds the TOAST threshold, so vectors are stored out of line by default unless storage is changed. Reductions: <code>halfvec</code> (2 bytes/dim, half the size, minimal recall loss), smaller-dimension models or Matryoshka truncation, binary quantization (<code>binary_quantize()</code> into <code>bit</code>) with re-ranking, and indexing a <code>halfvec</code> expression while storing full precision. pgvector indexes support up to 2,000 dimensions for <code>vector</code> and 4,000 for <code>halfvec</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','capacity','ai'] },
{ q: `How do you implement hybrid search (keyword + vector) in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<pre>WITH sem AS (
  SELECT id, row_number() OVER (ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1) AS r
  FROM chunks ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 50),
kw AS (
  SELECT id, row_number() OVER (ORDER BY ts_rank(search, q) DESC) AS r
  FROM chunks, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $2) q
  WHERE search @@ q ORDER BY ts_rank(search, q) DESC LIMIT 50)
SELECT id, sum(1.0 / (60 + r)) AS rrf
FROM (SELECT * FROM sem UNION ALL SELECT * FROM kw) s
GROUP BY id ORDER BY rrf DESC LIMIT 10;</pre><p>Reciprocal Rank Fusion merges two ranked lists without having to normalize incomparable scores. Keyword search catches exact terms (part numbers, names) that embeddings miss; vectors catch paraphrases.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','search','rag'] },
{ q: `What metadata and chunking decisions matter in a PostgreSQL-backed RAG system?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Chunk size/overlap tuned to the embedding model and content (headings-aware splitting beats fixed size).</li><li>Store <code>document_id</code>, <code>chunk_index</code>, source URL/page, <code>tenant_id</code>, access-control lists, language, <code>embedding_model</code> and version, content hash.</li><li>Content hash allows skipping re-embedding unchanged chunks on re-ingest.</li><li>Parent-child design: retrieve small chunks, return the surrounding section.</li><li>Index filter columns; enforce permissions in SQL (or RLS) <em>before</em> results reach the LLM.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['rag','data-modelling','ai'] },
{ q: `How do you scale pgvector, and when would you choose a dedicated vector database?`,
  a: `<p>Scale-up and scale-out within PostgreSQL: keep the HNSW index in RAM (size shared_buffers/instance accordingly), use <code>halfvec</code>/quantization, serve searches from read replicas, partition by tenant or category, and shard with Citus <span class="tag ext">extension</span> for very large corpora. A dedicated vector DB (Qdrant, Milvus, Pinecone, Weaviate) becomes attractive at hundreds of millions to billions of vectors with high QPS and strict latency, or when you need advanced features like built-in multi-vector, distributed ANN, or GPU indexing — at the cost of a second system and sync pipeline. Many products never reach that point.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','scaling','ai'] },
{ q: `How do you migrate to a new embedding model without downtime?`,
  a: `<ol><li>Add a new column (e.g. <code>embedding_v2 vector(1024)</code>) or a separate table keyed by chunk and model version — vectors from different models are not comparable.</li><li>Backfill in throttled batches; build the HNSW index on the new column (CONCURRENTLY).</li><li>Embed incoming documents with both models during the transition.</li><li>Evaluate retrieval quality on a test set; shadow-read.</li><li>Switch queries to the new column (feature flag), then drop the old index and column.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','migrations','ai'] },
{ q: `Managed PostgreSQL (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure) vs self-managed — trade-offs?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Managed</strong>: automated backups/PITR, HA failover, minor patching, monitoring; less staff time. Limits: no superuser, restricted extensions list, limited config and OS access, version lag, cost premium, vendor-specific failover behaviour and maintenance windows. <strong>Self-managed</strong> (VMs or Kubernetes with Patroni/CloudNativePG, pgBackRest): full control, any extension (Citus, custom), often cheaper at scale, but you own HA, backups, upgrades and on-call. Choice depends on team expertise, compliance (data location, keys), and extension needs.</p>`,
  tags: ['cloud','operations'] },
{ q: `How is Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL architecturally different from community PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>Aurora keeps the PostgreSQL query engine but replaces the storage layer: compute nodes send WAL (redo) records to a distributed, log-structured storage service replicated 6 ways across 3 AZs, which materializes pages itself. There are no checkpoints writing full pages, readers share the same storage (replica lag typically tens of ms, no WAL replay of data files), and failover is fast. Trade-offs: proprietary, I/O-priced (or I/O-Optimized tier), version lag, and some behaviours differ (e.g. no physical replication to self-managed PostgreSQL). It is wire- and SQL-compatible, not identical.</p>`,
  tags: ['cloud','aurora'] },
{ q: `Should you run PostgreSQL on Kubernetes?`,
  a: `<p>It is now mainstream with mature operators: <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> CloudNativePG (CNCF), Crunchy PGO, Zalando postgres-operator (Patroni-based). Operators handle bootstrap, replicas, failover, backups to object storage, rolling minor upgrades and pooler deployment. Requirements: reliable persistent volumes (local NVMe with anti-affinity, or good network storage), resource requests/limits tuned (avoid CPU throttling and memory limits that OOM-kill the postmaster), PodDisruptionBudgets, and a team comfortable with both Kubernetes and PostgreSQL internals. If the rest of your platform is on Kubernetes, it unifies operations; otherwise VMs or a managed service are simpler.</p>`,
  tags: ['kubernetes','operations'] },
{ q: `What storage characteristics matter for PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>fsync latency</strong> on the WAL volume — directly limits commit latency and single-session TPS.</li><li><strong>Random read IOPS/latency</strong> for working sets larger than RAM.</li><li><strong>Throughput</strong> for sequential scans, vacuum, backups, and checkpoints.</li></ul><p>Local NVMe offers ~100 µs latency and huge IOPS but data is lost with the instance (rely on replicas/backups). Network block storage (EBS gp3/io2, Persistent Disk) is durable and resizable but has ms latency and provisioned IOPS caps — check if you hit them (CloudWatch <code>VolumeQueueLength</code>, PG 18 AIO helps). Never use storage that lies about fsync (write caches without battery/power-loss protection).</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','io','capacity'] },
{ q: `How do you do capacity planning for a PostgreSQL cluster?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Track trends: data size growth per month (tables + indexes + WAL), QPS/TPS, CPU, memory hit ratio, IOPS, connections, replication lag.</li><li>Working set: does the hot data + indexes fit in RAM? If the hit ratio declines as data grows, plan RAM or archiving.</li><li>Headroom: target &lt;60–70% CPU at peak to survive failover (all traffic on fewer nodes) and spikes.</li><li>Disk: keep &gt;30% free — VACUUM FULL, index rebuilds, WAL spikes and replication slot retention all need space.</li><li>Operational limits: backup/restore time vs RTO, vacuum duration of the biggest table, XID consumption rate vs freeze.</li><li>Load-test with production-like data (pgbench custom scripts) before big launches.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['capacity','operations'] },
{ q: `What does a good PostgreSQL observability stack include?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Metrics</strong>: postgres_exporter + Prometheus/Grafana (or Datadog, pganalyze, PMM <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span>s): connections by state, TPS, cache hit ratio, replication lag and slot retention, XID age, dead tuples, checkpoints, temp files, locks, wait events.</li><li><strong>Query analytics</strong>: <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pg_stat_statements snapshots; <code>auto_explain</code> for slow plans.</li><li><strong>Logs</strong>: <code>log_min_duration_statement</code>, <code>log_lock_waits</code>, <code>log_autovacuum_min_duration</code>, <code>log_checkpoints</code> (on by default since 15), <code>log_temp_files</code>, parsed with pgBadger.</li><li><strong>Host</strong>: CPU, memory, disk latency/IOPS, free space.</li><li><strong>Alerts</strong> tied to runbooks (lag &gt; N, XID age &gt; 1B, disk &gt; 80%, idle-in-transaction &gt; 10 min).</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['monitoring','operations'] },
{ q: `How do you design multi-tenant vector search securely?`,
  a: `<p>Store <code>tenant_id</code> (and document ACLs) on every chunk row, enforce with RLS policies driven by a transaction-local setting, and make sure the ANN query still returns enough rows after filtering. For a few large tenants, partition by tenant (LIST or HASH) so each partition has its own HNSW index and searches only that tenant's graph; for many small tenants, a shared index with pgvector 0.8 iterative scans or a B-tree on <code>tenant_id</code> with exact distance on the small subset works well. Never rely on the prompt to the LLM for access control — filter in SQL before retrieval.</p>`,
  tags: ['pgvector','multi-tenant','security'] },
{ q: `What is the expand/contract (parallel change) pattern for schema migrations?`,
  a: `<p>Zero-downtime schema changes split a breaking change into backwards-compatible steps across deploys:</p><ol><li><strong>Expand</strong>: add the new column/table (nullable, no long locks); deploy code that writes to both old and new.</li><li><strong>Migrate</strong>: backfill existing rows in small batches.</li><li>Switch reads to the new structure; verify.</li><li><strong>Contract</strong>: stop writing the old column, then drop it in a later release.</li></ol><p>Every step must work with both the previous and next app versions, because rolling deploys run them simultaneously. Each DDL statement runs with a short <code>lock_timeout</code> and retries.</p>`,
  tags: ['migrations','deployments'] },
{ q: `How do blue-green deployments work for databases?`,
  a: `<p>A "green" copy of the database is built (for an upgrade, parameter change or major schema change), kept in sync with "blue" via logical replication, tested, and then traffic is switched: stop writes on blue, wait for green to catch up, sync sequences, switch endpoints, optionally keep reverse replication for rollback. AWS RDS Blue/Green Deployments automate this using logical replication. Constraints are those of logical replication: no DDL replication during the window, primary keys required, large objects not replicated.</p>`,
  tags: ['deployments','upgrades','replication'] },
{ q: `Walk through a near-zero-downtime major upgrade using logical replication.`,
  a: `<ol><li>Provision the new-version cluster; apply schema with <code>pg_dump --schema-only</code>.</li><li>On the old primary: <code>CREATE PUBLICATION upg FOR ALL TABLES;</code> (ensure every table has a PK or replica identity).</li><li>On the new cluster: <code>CREATE SUBSCRIPTION upg CONNECTION '...' PUBLICATION upg;</code> — initial copy then streaming. (Alternatively <code>pg_createsubscriber</code> from a physical standby to skip the copy.)</li><li>Monitor <code>pg_stat_subscription</code> lag; freeze DDL.</li><li>Cutover: pause writers (or set the old DB read-only), wait for zero lag, copy sequence values with <code>setval()</code>, run <code>ANALYZE</code>, repoint apps.</li><li>Optional rollback path: reverse subscription from new to old.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['upgrades','replication','migrations'] },
{ q: `How can logical replication consolidate or split databases?`,
  a: `<p>Logical replication works per table with row and column filters, so you can <strong>fan in</strong> several regional or microservice databases into one reporting database (multiple subscriptions into one subscriber; tables must not collide, or use different schemas), or <strong>fan out</strong>: split a monolith by publishing subsets of tables (or rows, e.g. <code>WHERE (tenant_id IN (...))</code>) to new clusters during a service extraction or tenant move. Each subscription uses a replication slot on the source — monitor retained WAL.</p>`,
  tags: ['replication','logical','migrations'] },
{ q: `Describe a CDC pipeline from PostgreSQL to Kafka, and its risks.`,
  a: `<p>Debezium <span class="tag tool">third-party tool</span> (Kafka Connect) creates a logical replication slot with the <code>pgoutput</code> plugin, performs an initial snapshot, then streams row-level change events per table into Kafka topics. Requirements: <code>wal_level = logical</code>, a publication, REPLICA IDENTITY appropriate for the needed before-images. Risks: if the connector stops, the slot retains WAL until the disk fills — set <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> and alert on slot lag; low-traffic databases may need heartbeat events so the slot advances; failover lost slots before PG 17 failover slots; schema changes must be coordinated with consumers (schema registry).</p>`,
  tags: ['cdc','replication','kafka'] },
{ q: `How do you serve users across multiple geographies with PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Single primary in one region plus read replicas in other regions: fast local reads, writes pay cross-region latency (~70–250 ms).</li><li>Reduce write round trips: batch statements, use stored procedures for multi-statement logic, avoid chatty ORMs across regions.</li><li>Partition users by home region (geo-sharding): each region's cluster owns its users' data; global data replicated via logical replication.</li><li>Edge caching for read-mostly content.</li><li>If you truly need multi-region writes with strong consistency, evaluate distributed SQL (Spanner, CockroachDB, YugabyteDB) or EDB PGD.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['multi-region','latency','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do you meet data-residency requirements (e.g. India data must stay in India)?`,
  a: `<p>Shard or deploy per jurisdiction: separate clusters (or Citus nodes / schemas pinned to nodes) in each region, with tenant-to-region routing in the app. Ensure backups, WAL archives, replicas, logs and CDC streams also stay in-region — a DR replica in another country can violate residency. Global reference data can be replicated outward; personal data should not. Document data flows, use per-region encryption keys, and enforce region tags in the tenant directory.</p>`,
  tags: ['compliance','sharding','multi-region'] },
{ q: `How is encryption handled in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>In transit</strong>: TLS (<code>ssl = on</code>, certificates), enforce with <code>hostssl</code> lines in pg_hba.conf and <code>sslmode=verify-full</code> on clients. PG 17 supports direct TLS negotiation (<code>sslnegotiation=direct</code>).</li><li><strong>At rest</strong>: community PostgreSQL has <strong>no built-in TDE</strong>; use filesystem/volume encryption (LUKS, cloud KMS-encrypted disks) and encrypted backups (pgBackRest encryption). Commercial forks (EDB, Percona's pg_tde extension, Cybertec) offer TDE.</li><li><strong>Column-level</strong>: <span class="tag ext">extension</span> pgcrypto (<code>pgp_sym_encrypt</code>) or, better, application-side encryption so keys never reach the database.</li><li>Passwords: <code>scram-sha-256</code>.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['security','encryption'] },
{ q: `Describe a least-privilege role architecture for an application database.`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>app_owner</code> (NOLOGIN): owns schemas and tables; used only by migrations (via <code>SET ROLE</code> from a migration login).</li><li><code>app_rw</code> (NOLOGIN): SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on app tables, USAGE on sequences; granted to the app login role.</li><li><code>app_ro</code>: SELECT only, for reporting and support.</li><li><code>ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE app_owner IN SCHEMA app GRANT ... TO app_rw;</code> so new tables are covered.</li><li>Monitoring login with <code>pg_monitor</code>; no app connects as superuser or table owner (owners bypass RLS and can DROP).</li><li><code>REVOKE ALL ON DATABASE ... FROM PUBLIC</code>, <code>REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC</code>, per-role timeouts and connection limits.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['security','roles'] },
{ q: `Queue in PostgreSQL vs a dedicated message broker?`,
  a: `<p>A PostgreSQL queue table with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> gives transactional enqueue (job created atomically with business data), simple operations, and handles hundreds to a few thousand jobs/s well. Libraries: pgmq <span class="tag ext">extension</span>, River, Graphile Worker, Solid Queue, Que. Costs: dead-tuple churn needing aggressive vacuum, polling load, and it competes with OLTP for resources. Choose Kafka/RabbitMQ/SQS for very high throughput, fan-out to many consumers, retention/replay of event streams, or cross-team integration. A common hybrid is the outbox pattern: transactional write in PostgreSQL, relay to a broker.</p>`,
  tags: ['queues','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do you implement idempotency for APIs using PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<pre>CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (
  key        text PRIMARY KEY,
  request_hash text NOT NULL,
  response   jsonb,
  created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now());</pre><p>In the same transaction as the business operation: <code>INSERT INTO idempotency_keys(key, request_hash) VALUES ($1,$2) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code>; if no row was inserted, return the stored response (or 409 if the request hash differs or the first request is still in progress); otherwise perform the work, store the response, and commit. Purge keys older than the retry window via partitions or a batch job.</p>`,
  tags: ['patterns','idempotency'] },
{ q: `Sagas vs two-phase commit for transactions across services?`,
  a: `<p>Two-phase commit (<code>PREPARE TRANSACTION</code>) gives atomicity across databases but needs a reliable coordinator, holds locks while waiting, and orphaned prepared transactions block vacuum — rarely used across microservices. <strong>Sagas</strong> split the business transaction into local transactions, each publishing an event (via outbox); failures trigger compensating actions (refund, release stock). Sagas accept eventual consistency and require idempotent, retryable steps and clear compensation logic. Within one PostgreSQL database, simply use one transaction.</p>`,
  tags: ['distributed','transactions','microservices'] },
{ q: `Should you store files (PDFs, images) inside PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>Usually no. Store files in object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob) and keep metadata in PostgreSQL: key, size, content type, checksum, owner, created_at. Large <code>bytea</code> values bloat the database, backups and replication, consume shared buffers and WAL, and make restores slow; object storage is cheaper and serves files directly via signed URLs. Exceptions: small files (&lt; a few hundred KB) that need strict transactional consistency with rows. Large Objects (<code>lo_*</code>) avoid the 1 GB bytea limit but have awkward permissions and cleanup (<code>vacuumlo</code>), and are not replicated logically.</p>`,
  tags: ['storage','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `When would you consider a PostgreSQL-compatible distributed SQL database instead?`,
  a: `<p>When you need horizontal write scaling <em>and</em> strong consistency across nodes or regions with automatic rebalancing and survival of zone/region failure without manual promotion — e.g. global financial ledgers or multi-region SaaS with strict RPO 0. Candidates: CockroachDB, YugabyteDB, Google Spanner (PostgreSQL interface), AlloyDB/Aurora (still single-writer). Costs: higher per-transaction latency (consensus), different performance characteristics (contention on hot keys, retries), and partial compatibility — many extensions (PostGIS variants, pgvector support varies), some catalog features and isolation behaviours differ. Benchmark your actual workload first.</p>`,
  tags: ['distributed-sql','scaling'] },
{ q: `Your traffic will grow 10x in a year. How do you plan the database architecture?`,
  a: `<ol><li>Measure current bottlenecks: top queries by total time, CPU, IO, connections, write rate, data growth.</li><li>Cheapest wins first: query/index tuning, pooling, caching, moving reads to replicas, moving analytics off OLTP.</li><li>Scale up: bigger instance, NVMe/IO-optimized storage, memory for the working set.</li><li>Reduce data: partition and archive cold data; keep the hot set small.</li><li>Prepare for scale-out: pick and add the shard key (<code>tenant_id</code>) to tables, indexes and queries now, even on one node — then Citus or app-level sharding becomes a migration, not a rewrite.</li><li>Load-test at 10x with production-like data; validate failover and restore times at the new size.</li></ol>`,
  tags: ['scaling','capacity'] },
{ q: `How should an application route queries between primary and replicas?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Explicit routing in the data layer: separate connection pools for <code>writer</code> and <code>reader</code>; mark read-only transactions (Rails <code>connects_to</code>, Spring <code>@Transactional(readOnly=true)</code> with a routing data source).</li><li>Lag awareness: exclude replicas whose <code>now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp()</code> or LSN distance exceeds a threshold; route post-write reads to the primary.</li><li>Infrastructure routing: HAProxy with Patroni health endpoints (<code>/replica?lag=10MB</code>), PgCat/Pgpool-II query routing, or libpq <code>target_session_attrs=prefer-standby</code>.</li><li>Do not let SQL parsing decide transparently for functions with side effects.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['read-replicas','routing'] },
{ q: `Database-per-service vs shared database in microservices?`,
  a: `<p><strong>Database per service</strong> (separate database or at least separate schema with exclusive ownership): services evolve schemas independently, failures and load are isolated, and ownership is clear; cross-service queries go through APIs or replicated read models (CDC), and consistency becomes eventual (sagas, outbox). <strong>Shared database</strong>: simple joins and transactions, but tight coupling — one team's migration or slow query breaks others. Pragmatic middle ground: one PostgreSQL cluster, one schema and role per service, no cross-schema access except via published views — and split clusters later when load demands.</p>`,
  tags: ['microservices','architecture'] },
{ q: `How would you implement event sourcing on PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<pre>CREATE TABLE events (
  stream_id   uuid        NOT NULL,
  version     int         NOT NULL,
  event_type  text        NOT NULL,
  payload     jsonb       NOT NULL,
  occurred_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  global_seq  bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,
  PRIMARY KEY (stream_id, version));</pre><p>The <code>(stream_id, version)</code> primary key gives optimistic concurrency: appending version N+1 fails if another writer already did. Projections read by <code>global_seq</code>, but sequence values can commit out of order, so consumers must handle gaps (track committed positions or use logical decoding). Add snapshots per stream for long streams and partition by time for archival.</p>`,
  tags: ['event-sourcing','patterns','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `What are the options for modelling hierarchical data in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Adjacency list</strong> (<code>parent_id</code>, like <code>employees.manager_id</code>) queried with <code>WITH RECURSIVE</code>: simple writes, recursive reads; PG 14 added <code>SEARCH</code> and <code>CYCLE</code> clauses.</li><li><strong>Materialized path</strong> with <span class="tag ext">extension</span> ltree (<code>'company.eng.platform'</code>): fast subtree queries with GiST index (<code>path &lt;@ 'company.eng'</code>), moves rewrite paths.</li><li><strong>Closure table</strong>: all ancestor-descendant pairs; fast reads, more storage and write work.</li><li><strong>Nested sets</strong>: fast reads, very expensive writes — rarely worth it today.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','hierarchies'] },
{ q: `How do you model temporal (versioned/effective-dated) data in PostgreSQL?`,
  a: `<p>Store validity as a range: <code>valid_during tstzrange</code>, and prevent overlapping versions of the same entity with an exclusion constraint using <span class="tag ext">extension</span> btree_gist: <code>EXCLUDE USING gist (product_id WITH =, valid_during WITH &amp;&amp;)</code>. PG 18 adds core temporal keys: <code>PRIMARY KEY (product_id, valid_during WITHOUT OVERLAPS)</code> and temporal foreign keys with <code>PERIOD</code>. Query "as of" with <code>valid_during @&gt; timestamptz '2025-06-01'</code>. For audit history of every change, add a separate history table maintained by triggers.</p>`,
  tags: ['data-modelling','temporal','versions'] },
{ q: `What does a geospatial architecture with PostGIS look like?`,
  a: `<p><span class="tag ext">extension</span> PostGIS adds <code>geometry</code>/<code>geography</code> types, hundreds of spatial functions and GiST/SP-GiST/BRIN spatial indexes. Typical design: store features in SRID 4326 (or a projected SRID for metric calculations), GiST index on the geometry, use index-aware predicates (<code>ST_DWithin</code>, <code>ST_Intersects</code>) rather than computing <code>ST_Distance</code> on every row, KNN ordering with <code>&lt;-&gt;</code>. Serve map tiles via <code>ST_AsMVT</code> or pg_tileserv; partition large telemetry tables by time; simplify geometries for display. It is widely used in transport, utilities and infrastructure asset management.</p>`,
  tags: ['postgis','extensions','data-modelling'] },
{ q: `How do you design for very high write ingest (e.g. 100k rows/s)?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Batch: <code>COPY</code> or multi-row INSERTs (hundreds to thousands of rows per statement), not row-by-row commits.</li><li>Minimize indexes on the hot table (each index multiplies write work); BRIN instead of B-tree on time columns.</li><li>Partition by time so indexes stay small and old data drops cheaply.</li><li>Group commits: fewer, larger transactions; consider <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> for loss-tolerant telemetry.</li><li>Use an UNLOGGED staging table then move data in bulk when durability of in-flight data is not critical.</li><li>Tune <code>max_wal_size</code> to avoid checkpoint storms; <code>wal_compression</code>; fast WAL storage.</li><li>Shard (Citus) when one node's WAL throughput saturates.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['ingest','performance','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do you design a reporting replica without hurting the primary?`,
  a: `<p>Physical replicas face a dilemma: <code>hot_standby_feedback = on</code> bloats the primary during long reports; off means reports get cancelled by recovery conflicts. Options: a dedicated reporting standby with <code>max_standby_streaming_delay = -1</code> or several hours (replay waits for queries; the replica lags but the primary is unaffected) and feedback off; or a <strong>logical replica</strong> containing just the needed tables, which is independent (its own vacuum, extra indexes and materialized views allowed, no recovery conflicts). Beyond that, ship data to a warehouse.</p>`,
  tags: ['read-replicas','reporting'] },
{ q: `Why are chatty ORMs a major problem when the database is in another zone or region?`,
  a: `<p>Each statement is a network round trip. An endpoint issuing 200 queries (N+1 pattern) costs 200 × 0.5 ms = 100 ms in the same AZ but 200 × 70 ms = 14 s cross-region. Architectural fixes: place app servers in the same zone/region as the primary; batch with joins, <code>IN</code>/<code>= ANY($1)</code> array parameters, or eager loading; move multi-step logic into one statement or a function; use libpq pipeline mode (PG 14+) or asynchronous drivers to overlap round trips.</p>`,
  tags: ['latency','orm','architecture'] },
{ q: `How do you protect PostgreSQL backups against ransomware or a compromised account?`,
  a: `<ul><li>Immutable storage: S3 Object Lock (compliance mode) / Azure immutable blobs for backup repositories, with retention longer than detection time.</li><li>Separate cloud account/subscription and credentials for backups; the DB host can write but not delete.</li><li>Encrypt backups with keys stored separately (pgBackRest repository encryption, KMS).</li><li>Off-site copy in another region; periodic offline/air-gapped copy for critical data.</li><li>Monitor for mass deletes/updates and unusual role activity (pgAudit, log analysis).</li><li>Regular restore drills from the immutable copy specifically.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['backup','security','dr'] },
{ q: `What is HTAP and can PostgreSQL do it?`,
  a: `<p>Hybrid Transactional/Analytical Processing means running OLTP and analytics on the same data with fresh results. Plain PostgreSQL is row-oriented and optimized for OLTP; analytics on the primary competes for resources. Approaches: analytics on replicas (physical or logical), parallel query for moderate scans, BRIN and partitioning for time ranges, and columnar <span class="tag ext">extension</span>s (Citus columnar, TimescaleDB compression, pg_duckdb). Products like AlloyDB add a columnar engine. For heavy BI, CDC into a columnar warehouse remains the most robust architecture.</p>`,
  tags: ['olap','htap','architecture'] },
];

PGM.INTERVIEW.performance = [
{ q: `EXPLAIN vs EXPLAIN ANALYZE — what is the difference and what is the risk?`,
  a: `<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the chosen plan with the planner's <strong>estimates</strong> (cost, rows, width) without executing. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> actually <strong>runs</strong> the query and adds real timings, row counts and loops per node — the only way to see where estimates diverge from reality. Risk: it executes writes too, so wrap DML in <code>BEGIN; EXPLAIN ANALYZE UPDATE ...; ROLLBACK;</code>. Timing overhead can inflate fast nodes; <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, TIMING OFF)</code> reduces it. Always include <code>BUFFERS</code> (automatic with ANALYZE from PG 18).</p>`,
  tags: ['explain'] },
{ q: `How do you read the cost numbers in an EXPLAIN plan?`,
  a: `<p><code>cost=0.43..8.45 rows=1 width=72</code>: the first number is the <strong>startup cost</strong> (before the first row can be returned — high for sorts and hash builds), the second the <strong>total cost</strong> to return all rows. Units are arbitrary, based on <code>seq_page_cost</code> = 1.0, <code>random_page_cost</code> = 4.0, <code>cpu_tuple_cost</code> = 0.01, <code>cpu_index_tuple_cost</code> = 0.005, <code>cpu_operator_cost</code> = 0.0025. A parent's cost includes its children. Costs are only comparable between plans of the same query on the same server; they do not convert to milliseconds. <code>rows</code> is the estimated output rows; <code>width</code> the average row size in bytes.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','planner'] },
{ q: `How do loops affect actual time and rows in EXPLAIN ANALYZE?`,
  a: `<p>For a node executed multiple times (the inner side of a nested loop, or per parallel worker), <code>actual time</code> and <code>rows</code> are <strong>averages per loop</strong>. <code>Index Scan ... (actual time=0.010..0.012 rows=3 loops=50000)</code> means 150,000 rows total and ~0.6 s of time. Missing the multiplication is the classic misreading — a "fast" node with huge loops is often the real bottleneck. Tools such as explain.depesz.com and explain.dalibo.com compute exclusive times for you.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain'] },
{ q: `What does the BUFFERS option show and why is it important?`,
  a: `<p><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> reports per node: <code>shared hit</code> (found in shared buffers), <code>shared read</code> (read from OS cache or disk), <code>dirtied</code>, <code>written</code>, plus <code>temp read/written</code> for spills, and with <code>track_io_timing</code> the I/O time. It explains <em>why</em> a node is slow: 2 s with <code>read=150000</code> is I/O-bound (8 KB × 150k = 1.2 GB read), while 2 s with all hits is CPU-bound. It also reveals that a second run is fast only because data is now cached. PG 18 includes BUFFERS by default with ANALYZE.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','io'] },
{ q: `Why might PostgreSQL choose a sequential scan even though an index exists?`,
  a: `<ul><li>The query returns a large fraction of the table (often &gt; 5–10%) — reading the whole table sequentially is cheaper than random index lookups.</li><li>The table is small (a few pages).</li><li>The index cannot be used: function on the column (<code>lower(email)</code>), type mismatch or implicit cast, leading column of a composite index not constrained, <code>LIKE '%x'</code>, non-default collation for <code>LIKE 'x%'</code>.</li><li>Stale or wrong statistics overestimate matching rows.</li><li>Cost settings like <code>random_page_cost = 4</code> on SSD make index access look too expensive.</li></ul><p>Diagnose with <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>; test with <code>SET enable_seqscan = off</code> in a session only to compare costs, never as a fix.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','planner','explain'] },
{ q: `Index Scan vs Index Only Scan vs Bitmap Index Scan?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Index Scan</strong>: walks the index and fetches each matching heap tuple in index order — good for few rows or when order is needed (ORDER BY ... LIMIT).</li><li><strong>Index Only Scan</strong>: all needed columns are in the index and pages are all-visible in the visibility map, so heap access is skipped (check <code>Heap Fetches</code>).</li><li><strong>Bitmap Index Scan + Bitmap Heap Scan</strong>: collects matching TIDs into a bitmap sorted by physical location, then reads heap pages in order, each once — good for medium selectivity and for combining indexes with <code>BitmapAnd</code>/<code>BitmapOr</code>. Loses index ordering.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['indexes','explain'] },
{ q: `What do "lossy" bitmap and "Recheck Cond" mean?`,
  a: `<p>A bitmap normally stores exact tuple IDs. If it would exceed <code>work_mem</code>, it degrades to storing only <strong>page numbers</strong> ("lossy" blocks). Then every tuple on those pages must be rechecked against the condition — reported as <code>Heap Blocks: exact=1200 lossy=48000</code> and <code>Rows Removed by Index Recheck</code>. Many lossy blocks mean wasted CPU; increasing <code>work_mem</code> for that query makes the bitmap exact. Recheck is also always required for some index types (e.g. GIN with lossy operators, trigram searches).</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','work_mem'] },
{ q: `When does the planner choose Nested Loop, Hash Join or Merge Join?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Nested Loop</strong>: for each outer row, probe the inner side (ideally via an index). Best when the outer side is small; disastrous when the outer row estimate is badly low.</li><li><strong>Hash Join</strong>: builds a hash table on the smaller input, probes with the other. Best for large unsorted equi-joins; needs memory (<code>work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier</code>) or spills in batches.</li><li><strong>Merge Join</strong>: both inputs sorted on the join key (by index or sort) and merged. Good for very large inputs already sorted, and supports some non-hashable types.</li></ul><p>Bad join choices usually trace back to row misestimates, not to the join algorithms themselves.</p>`,
  tags: ['joins','planner','explain'] },
{ q: `A Hash node shows "Batches: 16". What does that mean?`,
  a: `<p>The hash table did not fit in its memory budget (<code>work_mem × hash_mem_multiplier</code>), so PostgreSQL partitioned both inputs into 16 batches written to temp files and processed them one at a time — extra disk I/O. <code>Memory Usage</code> shows the peak. Fixes: raise <code>work_mem</code> for that session/query, reduce the build side (filter earlier, select fewer columns — width matters), or ensure the smaller relation is the build side (fix estimates). If "Batches" grew from the original plan ("originally 1"), the planner underestimated rows.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','work_mem','joins'] },
{ q: `What does "Sort Method: external merge Disk: 245000kB" indicate?`,
  a: `<p>The sort exceeded <code>work_mem</code> and spilled ~240 MB to temp files, performing an external merge sort — much slower than an in-memory <code>quicksort</code>. <code>top-N heapsort</code> appears when a LIMIT allows keeping only N rows. Fixes: an index providing the order (no sort at all), a larger <code>work_mem</code> for that query (a spill of 240 MB usually needs a somewhat larger in-memory budget), selecting fewer/narrower columns, or adding LIMIT/keyset pagination. Track spills globally with <code>log_temp_files</code> and <code>temp_blks_written</code> in pg_stat_statements.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','work_mem','sorting'] },
{ q: `What does "Rows Removed by Filter" tell you?`,
  a: `<p>The node read rows and then discarded them because they failed a condition that was not used as an index condition. <code>Seq Scan on orders ... Rows Removed by Filter: 4999990</code> to return 10 rows signals a missing index. On an index scan, a large "Rows Removed by Filter" means the index matched only part of the predicate (e.g. index on <code>customer_id</code> but filter on <code>status</code> too) — a composite or partial index may be better. Compare it with the rows returned to judge efficiency.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','indexes'] },
{ q: `What is the Memoize node?`,
  a: `<p>Added in PG 14: placed above the inner side of a nested loop, it caches inner results keyed by the outer row's join parameter. When many outer rows share the same key (e.g. many order_items per product), repeated index lookups are served from the cache. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows <code>Hits</code>, <code>Misses</code>, <code>Evictions</code> and memory. Controlled by <code>enable_memoize</code>; it relies on good n_distinct estimates — a wrong estimate can make it thrash.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','joins'] },
{ q: `What is Incremental Sort?`,
  a: `<p>Added in PG 13: if input is already sorted on a prefix of the required keys (e.g. an index on <code>(customer_id)</code> and the query orders by <code>customer_id, order_date</code>), PostgreSQL sorts only within each group of equal prefix values. It needs less memory, returns the first rows sooner, and works well with LIMIT. EXPLAIN shows <code>Incremental Sort</code> with <code>Presorted Key: customer_id</code>.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','sorting'] },
{ q: `In a parallel plan, what does "Workers Planned: 4, Workers Launched: 1" mean?`,
  a: `<p>The planner expected 4 workers but only 1 could start, because the pools were exhausted: <code>max_parallel_workers</code> (default 8) or <code>max_worker_processes</code> (default 8, shared with logical replication and extensions) were in use by other queries. The query runs mostly on the leader and is much slower than planned. Fix by raising those limits (with CPU headroom) or reducing per-query parallelism (<code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code>) so concurrent queries share workers more predictably.</p>`,
  tags: ['parallel','explain'] },
{ q: `Which EXPLAIN options beyond ANALYZE and BUFFERS are useful?`,
  a: `<ul><li><code>VERBOSE</code>: output columns, schema-qualified names, remote SQL for FDWs.</li><li><code>SETTINGS</code>: non-default planner settings in effect.</li><li><code>WAL</code>: WAL records/bytes/full-page images generated by DML.</li><li><code>GENERIC_PLAN</code> (PG 16): show the generic plan for a query with <code>$1</code> placeholders without values.</li><li><code>SERIALIZE</code> (PG 17): include the cost of converting output to wire format (detoasting big values).</li><li><code>MEMORY</code> (PG 17): planner memory usage.</li><li><code>FORMAT JSON</code>: for visualizers.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['explain'] },
{ q: `What is auto_explain and how would you configure it in production?`,
  a: `<p>A contrib module (load with <code>shared_preload_libraries</code> or <code>session_preload_libraries</code>) that logs the plan of statements exceeding a duration — catching plans of real production queries with real parameters:</p><pre>auto_explain.log_min_duration = '1s'
auto_explain.log_analyze = on        # actual rows; adds overhead
auto_explain.log_timing = off        # reduce per-node timing overhead
auto_explain.log_buffers = on
auto_explain.log_nested_statements = on
auto_explain.sample_rate = 0.1</pre><p>Analyze mode instruments every query (not just slow ones), so use sampling and timing off on busy systems.</p>`,
  tags: ['explain','monitoring'] },
{ q: `How do CTEs affect performance since PostgreSQL 12?`,
  a: `<p>Before PG 12, every CTE was an optimization fence: materialized fully, with no predicate pushdown. Since PG 12, a non-recursive, side-effect-free CTE referenced <strong>once</strong> is inlined like a subquery, so filters and indexes apply. You can control it: <code>WITH x AS MATERIALIZED (...)</code> forces materialization (useful to compute an expensive result once or to fence a bad plan), <code>NOT MATERIALIZED</code> forces inlining even when referenced multiple times. Data-modifying and recursive CTEs are always materialized.</p>`,
  tags: ['cte','planner'] },
{ q: `What should random_page_cost be on SSD/NVMe and why?`,
  a: `<p>The default 4.0 models spinning disks where random reads are far slower than sequential. On SSD/NVMe (and when most data is cached), random reads are much closer to sequential, so values of <strong>1.1–1.5</strong> are common. Lowering it makes index scans and nested loops relatively cheaper, which typically produces better OLTP plans. Set it globally or per tablespace (<code>ALTER TABLESPACE ... SET (random_page_cost = 1.1)</code>). Change it with measurement, since it shifts many plans at once.</p>`,
  tags: ['planner','gucs','io'] },
{ q: `What are the common causes of bad row estimates and how do you fix them?`,
  a: `<ul><li><strong>Stale statistics</strong> after bulk loads → <code>ANALYZE</code>.</li><li><strong>Skewed data</strong> poorly captured by the sample → raise per-column statistics target.</li><li><strong>Correlated columns</strong> → <code>CREATE STATISTICS</code> (dependencies, mcv).</li><li><strong>Expressions/functions</strong> in WHERE get default selectivity (often 0.5% or 33%) → expression index (which gets its own statistics) or extended statistics on the expression.</li><li><strong>Parameters/generic plans</strong> → check plan_cache_mode.</li><li><strong>Multi-way joins</strong> where errors compound → simplify, or break up with temporary results.</li><li><strong>Temp tables</strong> never analyzed by autovacuum → ANALYZE manually.</li></ul>`,
  tags: ['statistics','planner'] },
{ q: `How do you choose the column order in a composite B-tree index?`,
  a: `<p>An index on <code>(a, b, c)</code> is efficient for predicates on a leading prefix: <code>a</code>, <code>a,b</code>, <code>a,b,c</code>. Rules of thumb: (1) columns used with <strong>equality</strong> first, then the column used for <strong>range</strong> or <strong>ORDER BY</strong> — e.g. <code>WHERE customer_id = $1 AND order_date &gt;= $2 ORDER BY order_date</code> needs <code>(customer_id, order_date)</code>; (2) among equality columns, put the one shared by most queries first; selectivity matters less than many believe. Columns after a range condition cannot narrow the scan, only filter. PG 18 skip scan helps when a low-cardinality leading column is unconstrained.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','btree'] },
{ q: `What is a covering index (INCLUDE)?`,
  a: `<pre>CREATE INDEX orders_customer_inc ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (status, total_amount);</pre><p>Non-key columns in <code>INCLUDE</code> are stored only in leaf pages, so a query that filters on <code>customer_id</code> and reads <code>status, total_amount</code> can use an Index Only Scan without heap visits. Unlike adding them as key columns, included columns do not affect ordering or uniqueness (useful for a <code>UNIQUE</code> index plus payload). Costs: bigger index, more write work, and HOT updates are lost if included columns change.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','index-only-scan'] },
{ q: `When do partial indexes help?`,
  a: `<p>A partial index covers only rows matching a predicate:</p><pre>CREATE INDEX orders_pending_idx ON orders (order_date) WHERE status = 'pending';</pre><p>If 1% of orders are pending, the index is ~1% of the size, stays in cache, and is cheap to maintain. The query must include a condition that implies the predicate (<code>WHERE status = 'pending' AND ...</code>; parameterized <code>status = $1</code> does not match a generic plan). Other uses: unique constraints on a subset (<code>UNIQUE (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL</code>), queue tables, excluding NULLs.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','partial-index'] },
{ q: `How do expression indexes work and when are they needed?`,
  a: `<p>When a query filters on an expression, an index on the bare column cannot be used. Index the expression itself:</p><pre>CREATE INDEX customers_email_lower ON customers (lower(email));
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE lower(email) = lower('A@x.com');</pre><p>The expression must be <code>IMMUTABLE</code> and match the query's expression exactly. ANALYZE collects statistics on indexed expressions, which also improves estimates. Common uses: case-insensitive search (or use the citext <span class="tag ext">extension</span> / nondeterministic collations), JSONB keys <code>((attributes-&gt;&gt;'brand'))</code>, date parts.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','expression-index'] },
{ q: `Why should foreign key columns usually be indexed?`,
  a: `<p>PostgreSQL automatically indexes primary keys and unique constraints, but <strong>not</strong> the referencing column of a foreign key. Without an index on <code>orders.customer_id</code>: deleting or updating a customer key scans the whole orders table (per row!) to check references or cascade, holding locks the whole time; and joins from customers to orders cannot use index nested loops. A query to find missing FK indexes compares <code>pg_constraint.conkey</code> with the leading columns of indexes in <code>pg_index</code>. Exception: tiny tables or references never deleted/joined from the parent side.</p>`,
  tags: ['indexes','foreign-keys'] },

];
